// ══ UGC NET Paper 1 — Unit I — Teaching Aptitude — Previous Year Questions ══
// Paper 1 bank: goes into PAPER1_QB (kept separate from Paper 2 MASTER_QB).
// Format: {unit,paper,no,q,opts,ans,exp}  (+ alt:[extra accepted option idx], bonus:1 = dropped by NTA, full marks to all)
// paper = PYQ paper key (see PAPER1_PAPERS in the website); no = question order inside that paper.

PAPER1_QB.push(

  // ── June 2026 · Set 1 ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun26_a',no:23,q:'The curriculum and pedagogy in school learning should be A. Holistic B. Integrated C. Enjoyable D. Flexible E. Engaging',opts:['A) A, B and D only','B) B, C and E only','C) A, C, D and E only','D) A, B, C, D and E'],ans:3,exp:'Modern pedagogical philosophy (echoed in NEP 2020) holds that curriculum and pedagogy should simultaneously be holistic, integrated, enjoyable, flexible, and engaging – none of these qualities is in tension with the others. Why the others fail: (A), (B) and (C) each omit qualities that NEP-2020-aligned pedagogy explicitly recommends together — there is no genuine tension between holistic, integrated, enjoyable, flexible, and engaging curricula, so no option that drops any of them can be more correct than the option retaining all five. Exam tip: \'All of the above\'-type list questions in Teaching Aptitude are common when the list comes from a single official policy document (here, NEP 2020) — if every item is a genuinely recommended quality with no internal contradiction, the \'all of the above\' option is usually correct.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun26_a',no:24,q:'Active learning can be promoted by _________.',opts:['A) Maintaining attention','B) Prompting awareness','C) Helping in theoretical knowledge','D) Asking students to examine the material from alternative points of view'],ans:3,exp:'Active learning is fundamentally about learners doing something with the material – analysing, questioning, applying it from multiple perspectives – rather than passively receiving it, which is exactly what option (D) describes. Why the others fail: (A) Maintaining attention and (B) prompting awareness are passive/receptive processes, not active engagement. (C) \'Helping in theoretical knowledge\' describes content delivery, not learner activity. Only (D) has the learner actively doing something (examining material from alternative viewpoints), which is the defining feature of active learning. Exam tip: For \'active learning\' questions, look for the option where the student is the grammatical subject performing an action (analyse, question, apply, examine) — options describing what the teacher or material does are passive-learning distractors.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun26_a',no:38,q:'Arrange the following stages of child development according to Jean Piaget in increasing order. A. Operational stage B. Pre operational stage C. Sensorimotor stage D. Concrete operational stage E. Preconceptual thought stage',opts:['A) E,C,B,A,D','B) C,E,B,D,A','C) C,E,A,B,D','D) E,C,D,A,B'],ans:0,exp:'Piaget\'s standard sequence is Sensorimotor $\\to$ Preoperational (which contains an early Preconceptual sub-phase) $\\to$ Concrete Operational $\\to$ Formal Operational. The listed options mix a main stage with named sub-stages, which makes a strict "increasing order" reading depend on how the sub-stages are nested in the source textbook; we report the official key\'s sequence here rather than second-guessing the intended textbook convention. Why the others fail: (B), (C) and (D) each place at least one stage out of the sequence implied by the official answer key. As noted in the Explanation, Piaget\'s four-stage theory (Sensorimotor $\\to$ Preoperational $\\to$ Concrete Operational $\\to$ Formal Operational) doesn\'t map cleanly onto this five-item list, since \'Preconceptual thought\' and \'Operational stage\' are sub-stages/labels rather than the four standard stage names — so treat this as a key-driven answer rather than a pure concept-recall one. Exam tip: When a question\'s official answer doesn\'t cleanly follow the textbook theory as usually taught, don\'t spend exam time re-deriving it from first principles — flag it, take the key\'s answer, and move on; a small number of NET Paper I questions each session are like this.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun26_a',no:39,q:'Who developed the social model of Role playing?',opts:['A) Donald Oliver','B) Fannie Shaftel','C) David Johnson','D) John Dewey'],ans:1,exp:'Fannie Shaftel (with George Shaftel) is credited with developing the Role Playing model for social studies/values education, used to help students explore social situations and decision-making through enactment. Why the others fail: (A) Donald Oliver is associated with jurisprudential/public-issues teaching models, not role playing. (C) David Johnson is known for cooperative learning models. (D) John Dewey is the broader father of experiential/progressive education, but not specifically the social model of role playing. Exam tip: Teaching-model \'who developed X\' questions are a matter of direct recall — keep a short table of model$\\to$developer pairs (Role Playing–Shaftel, Jurisprudential–Oliver, Cooperative Learning–Johnson & Johnson, Concept Attainment–Bruner) for quick revision.'},
  // ── 06 Jan 2026 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_c',no:6,q:'For motivating and remedial purposes, a teacher:',opts:['A) can beat her students.','B) can mildly beat her students.','C) can give some light punishments.','D) can not beat her students; it is taken as a crime.'],ans:3,exp:'Modern teaching aptitude and Indian education law both reject corporal punishment outright, regardless of intent (motivation) or degree (mild vs severe). The RTE Act, 2009 makes no exception for “motivational” or “remedial” beating – any physical punishment is prohibited and actionable, which is exactly what Option (D) states.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_c',no:8,q:'Select CORRECT statements: A. Learning is a time-pass activity ; B. Learning is selecting the appropriate response and connecting it with the stimulus. ; C. Learning is a pre-given insight ; D. Learning is a process of progressive behaviour adaptation ; E. Learning is a bye-product of heredity and genetics Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A and C Only','B) B, C and E Only','C) B and D Only','D) A, D and E Only'],ans:2,exp:'B and D both describe learning as an active, gradual, response-building process – consistent with mainstream behaviourist and connectionist theory. A, C and E each mischaracterise learning: as trivial/purposeless (A), as an innate/pre-given state rather than a process (C), or as governed by heredity rather than experience (E) – so only Option (C) is fully correct.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_c',no:9,q:'Select the CORRECT statements: A. Teaching is an interaction between a better knower and a lesser knower ; B. Teaching must have corporal punishments ; C. Teaching must be done by upper class and upper caste people ; D. Teaching leads to insight development in both teacher and the learner ; E. Everyone cannot be taught so learning must be for intelligent students only Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B, C and D Only','B) B, C and E Only','C) A and D Only','D) A, B, C, D and E'],ans:2,exp:'Statements A and D describe teaching in purely functional, inclusive terms – an interactive knowledge exchange that benefits both participants. B, C and E each introduce an exclusionary or coercive condition (corporal punishment, caste/class restriction, ability-based exclusion) that is inconsistent with the accepted, non-discriminatory definition of teaching tested by NTA – so Option (C) alone is correct.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_c',no:10,q:'Match List – I with List – II. [2@lList – I (Action): A. Teacher makes all decisions ; B. Students perform in pairs, one performs other gives feedback ; C. Students carry out teacher prescribed tasks ; D. Student takes full responsibility for the learning process]<br>[List – II (Teaching Method): I. Self-Teaching ; II. Practice ; III. Reciprocal ; IV. Command] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I','B) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','C) A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II','D) A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV'],ans:1,exp:'Mosston\'s Spectrum is organised along a single continuum of decision-making authority, moving from teacher-centred (Command) through partially learner-involved (Practice, Reciprocal) to fully learner-centred (Self-Teaching). Reading each action in List I for “who decides / who controls” cleanly identifies its position on that continuum, giving the mapping A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I – Option (B).'},
  // ── 05 Jan 2026 · Morning Shift 1 ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_a',no:6,q:'If a teacher asks a question about the height of Qutub Minar, then she is asking a question for',opts:['A) Reflection level','B) Understanding level','C) Memory level','D) Reasoning level'],ans:2,exp:'Asking “what is the height of X” demands nothing more than retrieving a stored numerical fact – there is no requirement to explain a relationship (Understanding) or apply the fact to a new, unfamiliar situation (Reflective). By definition this places the question squarely at the Memory level of teaching.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_a',no:7,q:'Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R [4pt] Assertion A: Intrinsic motivation is the most important factor for individual\'s learning. Reason R: A teacher cannot motivate enough such that the learner gets interested in learning. [4pt] In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below',opts:['A) Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A','B) Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','C) A is correct but R is not correct','D) A is not correct but R is correct'],ans:2,exp:'Assertion A is a well-supported claim in educational psychology. Reason R, however, makes an absolute and overly strong claim (“cannot motivate enough”), which contradicts the well-documented ability of teachers to stimulate interest via good pedagogy – hence R is false. Because R is false, it also cannot be a correct explanation of A, ruling out Options A and B.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_a',no:8,q:'For a general Lesson Plan, arrange the following in appropriate order: A. Assessment ; B. Selection of text-teaching material ; C. Recapitulation ; D. Content Delivery ; E. Formulation of Objectives Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) E, B, D, C, A','B) A, C, E, B, D','C) A, D, C, B, E','D) E, C, D, B, A'],ans:0,exp:'A lesson cannot be planned without first fixing objectives (E); once objectives are fixed, appropriate material is selected (B); the lesson is then delivered (D); learning is reinforced through recapitulation (C); and finally the extent of learning is checked through assessment (A). This gives the sequence E, B, D, C, A – exactly Option A.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_a',no:9,q:'Select the CORRECT statements. A. Task specific rubric makes scoring easier. ; B. Artificial Intelligence is the evolved intelligence in adult human beings. ; C. An assessment rubric can be used in individual assessment. ; D. Super Intelligence refers to general and strong artificial Intelligence surpassing human intelligence. ; E. Analytic Rubric is good for summative evaluation. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, C and D Only','B) B and E Only','C) A, B, C and D Only','D) C, D and E Only'],ans:0,exp:'Three of the five statements withstand scrutiny: task-specific rubrics do simplify scoring (A), rubrics are valid tools for individual assessment (C), and Superintelligence is correctly defined as AI surpassing human intelligence (D). Statement B confuses artificial intelligence with human cognitive evolution – a category error – and Statement E misassigns analytic rubrics (best for detailed formative feedback) to summative evaluation, which is more the domain of quicker holistic rubrics. Hence A, C, D Only is correct.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_a',no:10,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II [LIST-I (Function): A. To monitor the learning process of the learner ; B. To know cause and to provide remedies for learning difficulties ; C. First stage of evaluation, provides information about learners ; D. To find out to what extent instruction objectives have been achieved]<br>[LIST-II (Types of Evaluation): I. Placement ; II. Formative ; III. Diagnostic ; IV. Summative] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV','B) A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I','C) A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV','D) A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'],ans:2,exp:'Each function statement contains a keyword that uniquely identifies its evaluation type: “monitor” $\\rightarrow$ Formative; “cause and remedies” $\\rightarrow$ Diagnostic; “first stage, information about learners” $\\rightarrow$ Placement; “extent objectives achieved” $\\rightarrow$ Summative. This gives A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV, exactly Option C.'},
  // ── 02 Jan 2026 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_b',no:6,q:'Choose the correct statements among the following: A. Diagnosis of the learners is done in Inter-Active stage of teaching. ; B. Feedback and Testing of the learners are done in Post-Active stage of teaching. ; C. Fixing up goals and content are done in Post-Active stage of teaching. ; D. Selection of appropriate testing devices is done in Post-Active stage of teaching. ; E. Decision about strategies of teaching is done in Post-Active stage of teaching. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) C, D and E Only','B) A, C and D Only','C) A, B and D Only','D) A, B and E Only'],ans:2,exp:'The three-phase model divides a teacher\'s total professional activity along a timeline: plan, then execute, then evaluate. Anything that is a decision made before the class begins (goals, content, strategy) is pre-active; anything happening live in the classroom (questioning, diagnosing, reinforcing) is interactive; and anything to do with judging outcomes (testing, feedback, remedial action, and choosing the tools used for that judgment) is post-active.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_b',no:7,q:'Arrange the educational objectives of cognitive domain from lower order to higher order skills. A. Evaluation ; B. Synthesis ; C. Analysis ; D. Knowledge ; E. Comprehension Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B, C, D, E','B) D, E, C, B, A','C) A, B, C, E, D','D) B, C, D, E, A'],ans:1,exp:'Bloom\'s Taxonomy (cognitive domain) orders thinking skills from simple recall of facts to complex creation and judgment: Knowledge (remembering), Comprehension (understanding), Application (using), Analysis (breaking down), Synthesis (combining/creating), Evaluation (judging). The five objectives given in this question skip Application but otherwise follow the standard order exactly.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_b',no:8,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II [LIST-I: A. Concept Mapping ; B. Five E based Lesson Planning ; C. Stimulus-response based lesson planning ; D. Unit Plan]<br>[LIST-II: I. Graphical tools for organizing and representing knowledge ; II. Constructive Approach ; III. Systematic arrangement of subject matter ; IV. Behaviourist Approach] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV','B) A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III','C) A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV','D) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I'],ans:1,exp:'This question tests whether a candidate can trace each planning tool or strategy back to the learning theory or defining feature it embodies: Concept maps embody visual knowledge organisation; 5E lesson plans embody constructivist, learner-centred inquiry; stimulus-response planning embodies behaviourist conditioning; and a Unit Plan embodies systematic content sequencing rather than any particular learning theory.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jan26_b',no:10,q:'Which of the following is NOT the criticism of behaviourist model of teaching?',opts:['A) It doesn\'t take into account important internal processes that take place in mind.','B) It is not appropriate for higher order learning.','C) It is not appropriate for lower order learning.','D) Learning is sometimes beyond the association between stimulus and response.'],ans:2,exp:'Behaviourism (Watson, Pavlov, Skinner) explains learning purely through observable stimulus-response associations and reinforcement, without reference to internal mental states. Its practical strength lies precisely in lower-order, rote, drill-and-practice learning (multiplication tables, spelling, habit formation), so not suited to lower-order learning contradicts behaviourism\'s actual documented strength and is therefore the false criticism.'},
  // ── 31 Dec 2025 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec25_a',no:6,q:'Choose the correct statements from the following: A. The capacity to think about our own thinking is Metacognition. ; B. Metacognition is instrumental in controlling our own cognitive and motivational processes. ; C. Teaching people how to learn stems from the idea of fostering a sense of agency and responsibility within learners. ; D. Metacognition anyhow does not help in teaching people how to learn. ; E. Metacognition knowledge and self-regulation are related components. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, D and E Only','B) A, C and D Only','C) B, D and E Only','D) A, B, C and E Only'],ans:3,exp:'Metacognition is standardly defined as "thinking about one\'s own thinking" – statement A is a textbook-correct definition. It governs self-regulation of cognitive and motivational processes (B correct). Modern pedagogy on "teaching people how to learn" explicitly ties into learner agency and responsibility (C correct). Metacognitive knowledge (what one knows about cognition) and regulation (how one controls it) are the two classical related components of metacognition (E correct).'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec25_a',no:7,q:'Arrange the following psychomotor domains of development from simple level to more complex one: A. Perception B. Set C. Guided response D. Mechanism E. Complex overt response',opts:['A) A, B, C, D, E','B) A, C, D, E, B','C) A, B, D, E, C','D) B, D, A, C, E'],ans:0,exp:'Simpson\'s Psychomotor Domain (1972) has the following ascending hierarchy: Perception $\\to$ Set $\\to$ Guided Response $\\to$ Mechanism $\\to$ Complex Overt Response $\\to$ Adaptation $\\to$ Origination. The first five levels exactly match A, B, C, D, E in that order. Correct Answer: Option A – A, B, C, D, E'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec25_a',no:8,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II: [LIST-I: A. Reliability ; B. Validity ; C. Objectivity ; D. Norms]<br>[LIST-II: I. Minimization of Personal Error ; II. Minimization of Constant Error ; III. Minimization of Variable Error ; IV. Minimization of Interpretive Error]',opts:['A) A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV','B) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','C) A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I','D) A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III'],ans:0,exp:'Reliability = consistency of measurement across repeated administrations $\\Rightarrow$ minimizes random/variable error (III). Validity = the test measures what it claims to measure $\\Rightarrow$ minimizes constant/systematic error (II). Objectivity = scorer-independence $\\Rightarrow$ minimizes personal error (I). Norms = a standard comparison framework $\\Rightarrow$ minimizes interpretive error (IV) when reading raw scores.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec25_a',no:9,q:'Which of the following behavioural ability is associated with affective domain of a learner?',opts:['A) Evaluation','B) Synthesis','C) Characterization','D) Skilled movements'],ans:2,exp:'Evaluation and Synthesis belong to Bloom\'s cognitive domain. Skilled movements belong to the psychomotor domain. Characterization (by a value or value complex) is the highest level of Krathwohl\'s affective domain – values become internalized and control the person\'s behaviour.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec25_a',no:10,q:'Which of the following is NOT correct about the Criterion-Referenced Testing?',opts:['A) It is also called Domain-Referenced Testing.','B) It is also called Objective-Referenced Testing.','C) Its principal uses are in mastery tests.','D) It is useful in testing the relative position of an individual in a group.'],ans:3,exp:'Criterion-Referenced Tests (CRT) measure performance against a fixed, predefined standard/criterion (mastery of a domain), not against other test-takers. It is legitimately called Domain-Referenced or Objective-Referenced testing, and is principally used for mastery testing. Testing the relative position of an individual within a group is the defining feature of Norm-Referenced Testing (NRT), not CRT. Correct Answer: Option D'},
  // ── 27 Jun 2025 · Morning Shift 1 ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_b',no:6,q:'Which of the following assessment types would require to carry out an activity or produce a product in order to demonstrate learning?',opts:['A) Formative Assessment','B) Portfolio Assessment','C) Summative Assessment','D) Performance Assessment'],ans:3,exp:'Performance Assessment directly requires the learner to perform a task or create a product as the evidence of learning, which is exactly the criterion given in the question. The other three options are defined by timing (formative/summative) or by being a compiled record of multiple products (portfolio), not by a single demonstrative activity/product. Why the others fail: (A) Defined by its purpose and timing (ongoing feedback during learning), not by requiring a product/activity; it can be as simple as an oral question. (B) A close distractor since a portfolio does contain products, but a portfolio is a collection over time, not a single activity performed to demonstrate learning in the moment; students often confuse this with performance assessment. (C) Defined by timing (end-of-unit/course) and grading purpose; a summative test can be a simple written exam with no activity or product required.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_b',no:7,q:'Which of the following is true regarding extrinsic motivation?',opts:['A) It is based on factors not related to the activity itself.','B) It is the natural tendency to seek out personal interests.','C) It is the natural tendency to conquer challenges.','D) It motivates to do something when we don\'t need to do.'],ans:0,exp:'Extrinsic motivation is, by definition, driven by factors external to (not related to) the task itself – such as marks, rewards, recognition, or avoiding punishment. Option (A) states this definition directly and correctly, while the other options describe features of intrinsic motivation or an unrelated, non-standard claim. Why the others fail: (B) "Natural tendency to seek out personal interests" describes intrinsic motivation, which originates from within the person, not extrinsic motivation. (C) "Natural tendency to conquer challenges" also describes an internally driven urge for mastery/competence, a hallmark of intrinsic motivation. (D) This is a distractor phrase with no standard basis in motivation theory; it does not describe either intrinsic or extrinsic motivation accurately.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_b',no:8,q:'In the light of cognitive domain, arrange the following classroom questions (A–E) in the correct ascending order – A. Understanding B. Evaluating C. Analyzing D. Applying E. Remembering Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) E, A, D, C, B','B) A, B, C, D, E','C) B, C, A, E, D','D) C, E, B, A, D'],ans:0,exp:'Placing the five cognitive processes on Bloom\'s fixed hierarchy from simplest (recall of facts) to most complex (making judgments) gives Remembering, Understanding, Applying, Analyzing, Evaluating – i.e. E, A, D, C, B – which is Option (A). Why the others fail: (B) Simply follows the alphabetical labelling order of the statements, not the actual cognitive hierarchy. (C) Places Evaluating (the second-highest level) first, which reverses the intended ascending sequence. (D) Mixes the hierarchy levels with no consistent ascending or descending logic.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_b',no:9,q:'Which of the following are the basic objectives of Affective Domain? A. Showing some new behaviour as a result of experience. B. Acting consistently with the new value. C. Attending to something in the environment. D. Showing some definite involvement or commitment. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A and B Only','B) C and D Only','C) A, B, C and D','D) B and C Only'],ans:2,exp:'Each of the four statements accurately reflects a recognised outcome of affective-domain learning – from simply attending to a stimulus (Receiving), to valuing and acting on it consistently (Valuing), to full commitment and behavioural change (Characterization, and the general outcome described in A). Since none of the four statements is false, the complete set A, B, C and D is the correct answer. Why the others fail: (A) Wrongly excludes C and D, which are also genuine affective-domain objectives (Receiving and Characterization respectively). (B) Wrongly excludes A and B, which correctly describe general behaviour change and the Valuing level. (D) Wrongly excludes A and D, both of which are legitimate affective outcomes (general behaviour change and Characterization).'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_b',no:10,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>LIST-I (Cognitive domain level)</th><th>LIST-II (Suggested word)</th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>Evaluation</td><td>I.</td><td>Define</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>Knowledge</td><td>II.</td><td>Appraise</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>Analysis</td><td>III.</td><td>Distinguish</td></tr><tr><td>D.</td><td>Application</td><td>IV.</td><td>Demonstrate</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV','B) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','C) A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III','D) A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III'],ans:0,exp:'Matching each cognitive level to its standard textbook action verb – Knowledge/Define, Application/Demonstrate, Analysis/Distinguish, Evaluation/Appraise – gives the combination A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV, i.e. Option (A). Why the others fail: (B) Swaps Evaluation and Knowledge, wrongly pairing Evaluation with "Define" (a Knowledge-level verb) and Knowledge with "Appraise" (an Evaluation-level verb). (C) Correctly pairs A and B but swaps Analysis and Application, wrongly linking Analysis with "Demonstrate" and Application with "Distinguish". (D) Contains both swaps together (A/B swapped and C/D swapped), making every pairing incorrect.'},
  // ── 26 Jun 2025 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_d',no:6,q:'Which of the following is one of the advantages of behavioural objectives that determine Teaching and Learning in Higher Education?',opts:['A) These are teacher-centred.','B) These make learning focused and achievable.','C) These focus too narrowly on minutiae.','D) These limit opportunities from spontaneous unintended outcomes occurring during learning experience.'],ans:1,exp:'Behavioural objectives translate broad educational aims into specific, observable, measurable performance statements. Because the target is explicit, both instructional planning and learner effort become sharply focused, and success becomes easier to verify – this is precisely what option (B) states. The remaining three options each restate well-known critiques of the behavioural-objectives model (rigidity, narrowness, and suppression of unplanned/emergent learning), so none of them can be classified as an "advantage." Why the others fail: (A) Being teacher-centred is usually raised as a criticism of behavioural objectives (the teacher pre-decides everything), not a benefit. (C) This is a classic disadvantage – excessive fragmentation of learning into trivial, disconnected behaviours. (D) This is also a disadvantage – rigid pre-specification can suppress valuable incidental learning.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_d',no:7,q:'Which of the following is the strategy that support motivation?',opts:['A) Connections across the curriculum.','B) Attributions to intellect rather than effort.','C) Work that is too much difficult.','D) Emphasis on finishing, not learning.'],ans:0,exp:'When learners can see how a topic connects to other subjects and to real life, the learning gains relevance and purpose, which is one of the most reliable ways to sustain intrinsic motivation. The other three practices each violate a core motivational principle: praising/attributing outcomes to fixed intellect discourages effort-based persistence; setting work that is too difficult produces frustration and learned helplessness; and emphasising finishing rather than understanding promotes shallow, performance-goal behaviour instead of mastery-goal behaviour. Hence option (A) is the only genuine motivation-supporting strategy. Why the others fail: (B) Fixed-ability attributions reduce persistence; effort-based attributions are what motivation theory recommends instead. (C) Excessive difficulty produces anxiety and disengagement rather than motivation; optimal challenge (not maximal difficulty) sustains motivation. (D) This promotes performance/completion goals over mastery goals, a well-known motivation-suppressing practice.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_d',no:8,q:'Arrange the steps of classroom action research A. Plan B. Reflect C. Action D. Observe E. Follow up Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) B, A, C, E, D','B) C, B, E, D, A','C) A, C, D, B, E','D) D, E, A, C, B'],ans:2,exp:'A classroom teacher-researcher must first plan an intervention (A), then act on it in the classroom (C), then systematically observe the effects of that action (D), then reflect on what the observations mean (B), and finally decide on follow-up steps or a refined next cycle (E). This gives the order A, C, D, B, E, which is option (C). Why the others fail: (A) Starts with Reflect, which cannot logically precede Plan; also places Follow up before Observe. (B) Starts with Action before any Plan exists, and ends with Plan, reversing the natural cycle. (D) Starts with Observe before any action has occurred to observe, which is logically impossible.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_d',no:9,q:'Which of the following are the characteristics of adolescent learners? A. Increased emotional sensitivity B. High Level of self-discipline C. Strong need for social belonging D. Cognitive development for abstract thinking E. Emotional validity Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A and D Only','B) B and C Only','C) D, C and E Only','D) A, C, D and E Only'],ans:3,exp:'Adolescent learners are developmentally characterised by emotional intensity, a strong drive toward peer/social belonging, and emerging abstract-reasoning ability – statements A, C, D and E all capture genuine, well-documented features of this stage. High self-discipline (B), however, is generally not a hallmark of adolescence; self-regulatory capacity is still under construction during these years, which is precisely why adolescents are often described as impulsive or inconsistent in self-control. Excluding B and retaining A, C, D, E gives option (D). Why the others fail: (A) Too restrictive; wrongly excludes C and E, which are also valid adolescent characteristics. (B) Wrongly includes B (high self-discipline is not typical of adolescence) and omits several valid characteristics. (C) Omits A (emotional sensitivity), which is also a genuine adolescent characteristic.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_d',no:10,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>LIST-I (Term)</th><th>LIST-II (Definition)</th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>Classroom Management</td><td>I.</td><td>Objectives focussing on attitudes and feelings.</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>Collaboration</td><td>II.</td><td>Memory and reasoning objectives.</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>Affective domain</td><td>III.</td><td>Techniques used to maintain a healthy learning environment.</td></tr><tr><td>D.</td><td>Cognitive domain</td><td>IV.</td><td>A philosophy about how to relate to others.</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II','B) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','C) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','D) A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III'],ans:0,exp:'Each term maps uniquely onto its standard definition: classroom management is inherently about maintaining an orderly, healthy classroom climate (III); collaboration is a relational stance/philosophy toward others (IV); the affective domain by definition targets attitudes and feelings (I); and the cognitive domain by definition targets memory and reasoning (II). This gives A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II, which is option (A). Why the others fail: (B) Swaps Classroom Management with Collaboration\'s definition and the Affective/Cognitive domain definitions. (C) Applies the affective-domain definition to Classroom Management and the cognitive-domain definition to Collaboration – an internally inconsistent shift. (D) Assigns the cognitive-domain definition to Classroom Management and mismatches the remaining three pairs as well.'},
  // ── 26 Jun 2025 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_c',no:6,q:'The role of a teacher in the simulation model is that of _________.',opts:['A) Explaining','B) Designing','C) Describing','D) Chosing activity'],ans:0,exp:'In the Simulation Model of teaching, before and during the simulated activity, the teacher\'s central task is to explain – clarifying the rules of the simulated situation, the roles each learner will play, the objectives of the exercise, and later, explaining the significance of the outcomes generated by the simulation. This directly matches Option (A), "Explaining". The other three options describe roles more closely associated with lesson planning or passive narration rather than the active, clarifying role a teacher plays while running a simulation exercise. Why the others fail: (B) Describes a pre-active/planning-stage function (designing the simulation itself, its materials, and its structure) rather than the teacher\'s role during the live simulation activity as asked in the question. (C) Suggests a passive, narrative role of simply describing events, which does not capture the active, clarifying, rule-explaining function that defines the teacher\'s role in this specific model. (D) Refers to activity-selection, a planning-stage decision, not the specific functional role played by the teacher once the simulation is underway.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_c',no:7,q:'The CBCS (Choice Based Credit System) in higher education promotes :',opts:['A) Rigid Curriculum structure','B) Flexibility in course selection','C) Limited student choices','D) Only theoretical learning'],ans:1,exp:'The very name "Choice Based Credit System" signals its core purpose: to give students the flexibility to choose courses (core, elective, skill-based) according to their own interests and career goals, accumulating credits at a self-paced rhythm across disciplines. This is precisely what Option (B), "Flexibility in course selection", states. The other three options describe the rigid, restrictive, or narrowly theoretical systems that CBCS was specifically designed to replace, making them direct opposites of the correct answer. Why the others fail: (A) This describes the traditional annual/fixed-curriculum system that CBCS was introduced to move away from; it is the direct opposite of CBCS\'s purpose. (C) Directly contradicts the "Choice Based" element that gives the system its name; CBCS expands, not limits, student choice. (D) Incorrect because CBCS explicitly includes skill-enhancement and ability-enhancement (often practical/applied) courses alongside core theoretical papers, not theory alone.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_c',no:8,q:'Arrange the problem-solving strategy. (A) Generate many possible solutions (B) Determine how to implement the solution and evaluate the success of the solution (C) Make a decision (D) Evaluate each solution (E) Define the problem Choose the correct answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) B, E, A, D, C','B) E, A, D, C, B','C) A, B, C, D, E','D) C, A, B, E, D'],ans:1,exp:'Logical problem-solving must proceed from problem identification through to implementation: first the problem is defined (E), then a range of possible solutions is generated (A), each of these is evaluated (D), the best one is chosen through a decision (C), and finally that decision is implemented with its success evaluated (B). This gives the sequence E, A, D, C, B, which is Option (B). Any alternative ordering either begins with a stage other than problem-definition, or places decision-making before solution-evaluation, both of which violate the logical dependency of the process. Why the others fail: (A) Incorrectly starts with (B), the implementation/outcome-evaluation stage, which logically can only occur at the very end of the process, after a decision has already been made and a solution chosen. (C) Starts with solution-generation (A) before the problem has even been defined (E appears last), and places implementation (B) second, both of which are logically impossible orderings. (D) Begins with "Make a decision" (C) before any solutions have been generated or evaluated, and places "Define the problem" (E) fourth instead of first – a completely inverted and illogical sequence.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_c',no:9,q:'Learning for all implies : (A) Inclusive Education (B) Equitable Education (C) Inclusive and Equitable Education (D) Quality Education (E) Categorized based Education Choose the correct answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) A, B and D only','B) A and E only','C) C only','D) D only'],ans:2,exp:'"Learning for all" is a specific, well-established policy phrase (rooted in SDG-4) that is defined as requiring both inclusion and equity together – not either one in isolation, and not quality or categorisation, which are separate or contradictory ideas respectively. Since only statement C explicitly states "Inclusive and Equitable Education" as a combined phrase, and none of A, B, D, or E alone captures this complete meaning, the correct selection is "C only", i.e. Option (C). Why the others fail: (A) Incorrectly treats inclusion, equity, and quality as three separate, independently valid answers, when the question specifically tests recognition of the combined phrase (C) as used in SDG-4, not its individual components listed separately. (B) Incorrectly pairs inclusion (A, a valid partial concept) with categorised education (E, which is contrary to the inclusive spirit of "for all"); this combination is internally contradictory and incorrect. (D) Selects "Quality Education" alone, which – while a genuine and related SDG-4 element – does not by itself capture the "for all" (universality/access) dimension that the question is specifically asking about.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_c',no:10,q:'Match List - I with List - II : <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>List - I (Learning style)</th><th>List - II (Strengths)</th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>Convergent</td><td>I.</td><td>Creating theoretical model</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>Divergent</td><td>II.</td><td>Practical Application of ideas</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>Assimilation</td><td>III.</td><td>Carrying out plans and tasks that involves students in new experiences</td></tr><tr><td>D.</td><td>Accommodative</td><td>IV.</td><td>Imaginative ability and generation of ideas</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','B) A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV','C) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','D) A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II'],ans:2,exp:'Each of Kolb\'s four learning styles has one, and only one, characteristic strength associated with it in standard educational-psychology theory: Convergent learners excel at applying ideas practically (II), Divergent learners excel at generating imaginative ideas (IV), Assimilators excel at building theoretical models (I), and Accommodators excel at hands-on task execution in new situations (III). This complete and correct matching – A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III – is exactly what Option (C) states, making it the correct answer. Why the others fail: (A) Every single pairing here is mismatched relative to the correct associations – for example, it wrongly pairs Convergent with "Creating theoretical model" (that strength belongs to Assimilation). (B) Correctly pairs A-II (Convergent–Practical Application) but incorrectly pairs B, C, and D – Divergent is wrongly linked to "Creating theoretical model" (I) instead of its true strength, imaginative idea-generation (IV). (D) Wrongly assigns "Imaginative ability" (IV) to Convergent (whose actual strength is practical application) and "Creating theoretical model" (I) to Divergent (whose actual strength is idea-generation); none of the four pairings in this option is correct.'},
  // ── 25 Jun 2025 · Shift 2 ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_e',no:6,q:'The term Andragogy refers to _____.',opts:['A) Childhood Learning theory','B) Adolescence Learning theory','C) Adult Learning theory','D) Self-directed Learning theory'],ans:2,exp:'Andragogy is the theory and practice of teaching adult learners, formally developed by Malcolm Knowles as a counterpart to pedagogy (child-teaching). It is therefore correctly defined as “Adult Learning theory”. Learner\'s Characteristics (Unit I) distinguishes Pedagogy (Greek paid = child, agogus = leading – the art/science of teaching children) from Andragogy (Greek andr = adult – the art/science of helping adults learn), a term popularised by Malcolm Knowles.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_e',no:7,q:'Rearrange the sequence to write a lesson plan. [A. Lesson opening ; B. Preplanning ; C. Lesson Layout ; D. Closure of Lesson ; E. Extended practice] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) D, C, A, E, B','B) C, A, D, B, E','C) A, C, B, D, E','D) B, A, C, E, D'],ans:3,exp:'A lesson plan is written and delivered in the order Preplanning $\\to$ Lesson opening $\\to$ Lesson Layout $\\to$ Extended practice $\\to$ Closure of Lesson, i.e. B, A, C, E, D – exactly Option D. This mirrors the universally taught “Before class $\\to$ Beginning $\\to$ Middle (development) $\\to$ Practice $\\to$ End” structure of lesson design.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_e',no:8,q:'When a teacher uses Lecture Method, then [A. It provides new information to the students. ; B. It provides original research to the students which are generally not found in text books. ; C. It can dramatize important concepts and share personal insights. ; D. It is less effective method for promoting thought and changing students attitudes. ; E. It deals with critical analysis and problem solving.] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B and C Only','B) B, C and E Only','C) A, B, C and E Only','D) A, D and E Only'],ans:0,exp:'Standard teaching-aptitude sources credit the lecture method with efficiently transmitting new information (A), sharing original research/expertise beyond the textbook (B), and allowing a skilled lecturer to dramatize ideas and add personal insight (C). It is not typically associated with critical analysis/problem-solving (E, which belongs to discussion/inquiry-based methods) — hence only A, B and C form the correct set.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_e',no:9,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II [LIST-I (Types of knowledge): A. The purpose of assessment ; B. What to assess ; C. Assessment strategies ; D. Interpretation and Action-taking]<br>[LIST-II (Assessment Literacy): I. An educator assesses student learning and content specific knowledge ; II. Educators\' intention with assessment data ; III. relates to the educator\'s purpose with the assessment ; IV. associated with the purpose of assessment and deep rooted in the values and principles] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II','B) A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III','C) A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV','D) A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III'],ans:3,exp:'Working from the most literal textual match first (A – “purpose” – pairs almost word-for-word with IV, which explicitly mentions “purpose of assessment”), the remaining three pairs fall into place by elimination and meaning: B (intention regarding data) with II, C (the actual act of assessing) with I, and D (interpretation, which loops back to purpose) with III. This gives A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III, i.e. Option D.'},
  // ── 25 Jun 2025 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_a',no:6,q:'The role of a teacher in supporting experiential learning can\'t be of a/the ________.',opts:['A) Coach','B) Mentor','C) Tutor','D) Technology'],ans:3,exp:'The question asks which item is not a possible role of the teacher. Coach, Mentor and Tutor are all legitimate facilitative roles a teacher can adopt during experiential learning, whereas Technology is an instructional resource/tool rather than a role a human teacher performs. Hence Technology is correctly identified as the role the teacher cannot be. Why the others fail: (A) a coach role (guiding practice, giving feedback on performance) is a standard facilitative role in experiential learning; it does not answer the question. (B) mentoring (sharing experience-based wisdom, guiding reflection) is likewise a valid teacher role in this model. (C) tutoring (individualised support) is also a valid teacher role.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_a',no:7,q:'Which of the following is not considered as self-generated feedback?',opts:['A) Assurance','B) Previewing','C) Retracing','D) Observation'],ans:0,exp:'Previewing, retracing and observation are all active strategies a learner performs on their own work to monitor and correct it – textbook examples of self-generated feedback. Assurance, by contrast, denotes reassurance/confidence rather than an act of self-checking, so it does not belong to this set and is correctly identified as the exception. Why the others fail: (B) a genuine self-monitoring strategy (scanning ahead), so it does not answer the question. (C) a genuine self-monitoring strategy (reviewing earlier material to self-correct). (D) a genuine self-monitoring strategy (watching one\'s own performance for errors).'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_a',no:8,q:'Arrange the level of Bloom\'s Taxonomy from lower to higher order in the cognitive domain: A. Analyzing B. Understanding C. Applying D. Evaluating E. Remembering Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) B, E, C, A, D','B) E, B, C, A, D','C) E, B, C, D, A','D) E, B, D, A, C'],ans:1,exp:'Each of the five listed cognitive levels is placed at its correct position in the standard hierarchy: Remembering is the base level (recall of facts), followed by Understanding (comprehension), Applying (using knowledge in new situations), Analyzing (breaking information into parts) and finally Evaluating (making judgments) as the highest of the five given levels. The letter sequence E, B, C, A, D encodes exactly this order. Why the others fail: (A) incorrectly places Understanding (B) before Remembering (E), reversing the two lowest levels. (C) swaps Evaluating (D) and Analyzing (A), placing Evaluating below Analyzing, which reverses their true relative order. (D) places Evaluating (D) and Applying (C) out of order, disrupting the correct middle-to-top sequence.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_a',no:9,q:'What among the following are known as Assessment Instruments? A. Tests B. Examinations C. Rubrics D. Worksheets E. Quizzes Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B and E Only','B) B, C, D and E Only','C) D and E Only','D) A, B, D and E Only'],ans:3,exp:'Tests, Examinations, Worksheets and Quizzes are all formats through which a student actively produces a response that becomes evidence of learning – the defining feature of an assessment instrument. A rubric instead specifies the criteria and performance levels used to judge that evidence; it is a scoring/evaluation aid, not an instrument that collects a response. Hence the correct set excludes Rubrics, matching Option (D) (A, B, D and E Only). Why the others fail: (A) correctly excludes C but wrongly also excludes D (Worksheets), which is a genuine assessment instrument. (B) wrongly includes C (Rubrics) and wrongly excludes A (Tests). (C) far too restrictive; wrongly excludes both A (Tests) and B (Examinations).'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun25_a',no:10,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II. <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>LIST-I (Principle)</th><th>LIST-II (Implication)</th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>Encouraging student\'s deep approaches to learning objectives</td><td>I.</td><td>Feedback should be embedded within assessment practices.</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>The summative and formative assessments to be learning oriented</td><td>II.</td><td>Task design should be approached from a program-wide perspective.</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>Facilitating dialogic forms of feed back</td><td>III.</td><td>Greater sense of ownership by students</td></tr><tr><td>D.</td><td>Involving some personal student investment</td><td>IV.</td><td>A variety of assessment for student motivation.</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','B) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','C) A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I','D) A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV'],ans:1,exp:'Each principle is matched to the implication that logically follows from it: programme-wide task design supports deep learning (A-II); assessment variety sustains motivation and keeps assessment learning-oriented (B-IV); embedding feedback in assessment practice is what makes dialogic feedback possible (C-I); and personal investment in a task naturally produces ownership (D-III). This complete, non-overlapping mapping is captured only by Option (B). Why the others fail: (A) uses the "obvious" straight-line A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV pairing, but none of these letter-numeral pairs actually reflects the correct principle-implication logic. (C) swaps A and B\'s correct implications and misassigns C and D. (D) a fully scrambled mapping; none of the four pairs is correct.'},
  // ── Dec 2024 · Set 1 ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec24_a',no:6,q:'Which of the following are the characteristics of learner centered Approach? (i) It lays emphasis on skills ; (ii) It is centered on learner\'s needs ; (iii) It is centered on cooperative determination of subject matter ; (iv) Lays emphasis on variability of exposure Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (iii) and (iv) only','B) (i), (ii) and (iii) only','C) (i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)','D) (ii) and (iv) only'],ans:2,exp:'Option (C) is correct because every one of the four listed features – skill emphasis, learner-need centring, cooperative determination of subject matter, and variability of exposure – is a textbook characteristic of learner-centred (as opposed to teacher-centred) pedagogy. None of the four statements describes a teacher-centred feature, so none can be excluded.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec24_a',no:7,q:'Which of the following is a free and Open-source content collaboration framework based on Javascript that enables existing CMSs and LMSs to create richer content?',opts:['A) H5P','B) Plotagon','C) Mindmeister','D) Labster'],ans:0,exp:'H5P is the only listed tool that is (a) free and open-source, (b) built on JavaScript, and (c) designed specifically to let existing CMSs/LMSs create richer, interactive content – exactly matching the question stem. Unit I includes ICT-based Teaching Support Systems. Candidates are expected to know common digital tools used to enrich online/blended teaching: content-authoring frameworks, animation tools, mind-mapping tools and virtual-lab simulators.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec24_a',no:8,q:'A child of first class is trying to learn how to draw a circle on his own but is able to do with the help of his/her teacher. In the light of Vygotsky\'s theory, it highlights?',opts:['A) Assimilation','B) Adoption','C) Zone of proximal development','D) Maturation'],ans:2,exp:'The defining feature of ZPD is exactly what is described: a task the learner cannot do alone but can do with assistance. Since the question explicitly asks for the concept “in light of Vygotsky\'s theory,” and ZPD is Vygotsky\'s central contribution, Option (C) is correct.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec24_a',no:9,q:'Project method of teaching was first propounded by.',opts:['A) Crow and Crow','B) John Dewey','C) Robert Miller','D) Robert Mager'],ans:1,exp:'Among the four names, only John Dewey is historically linked with the philosophical foundation (pragmatism, learning-by-doing) from which the Project Method emerged, and this is the option confirmed correct by the official NTA answer key. The Project Method is a learner-centred, activity-based teaching method in which learners undertake a purposeful, real-life task (a “project”) to construct knowledge through doing.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec24_a',no:10,q:'SWAYAM is a programme initiated by.',opts:['A) Government of India','B) National Council of Education Research and Training','C) University Grants commission','D) Indira Gandhi National Open University'],ans:0,exp:'SWAYAM is a flagship Government of India programme under the Ministry of Education, designed to achieve the three cardinal principles of education policy – access, equity and quality. Bodies like UGC, NCERT and IGNOU participate as content/coordination partners, but the initiating authority is the Government of India itself.'},
  // ── 29 Aug 2024 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_g',no:6,q:'To teach a particular topic a teacher first gives the students to watch the pre-recorded video of the lecture at home and then engages the students in discussions and problem solving activities in the class. This is an example of :',opts:['A) Lecture-demonstration','B) Flipped classroom','C) Asynchronous learning','D) Synchronous learning'],ans:1,exp:'The scenario — pre-recorded video at home followed by discussion/problem-solving in class — is the defining structure of the flipped classroom, making Option (B) correct. The model “flips” the traditional order so that lower-order cognitive work (initial exposure to content) is done independently at home, while higher-order cognitive work (discussion, application, problem solving) is done collaboratively in class with the teacher present to facilitate and clarify doubts.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_g',no:7,q:'Which component of Baddeley\'s basic working memory model is involved in the task of Rapid repetition of the word like “See-Saw” ?',opts:['A) Visuospatial sketchpad','B) Bounded rationality','C) Phonological loop','D) Meta-cognition'],ans:2,exp:'Rapid repetition of a word such as “See-Saw” is a classic demonstration of articulatory rehearsal within the phonological loop – the subsystem of working memory dedicated to holding and refreshing verbal/acoustic information through subvocal repetition. Hence Option (C) is correct.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_g',no:8,q:'A teacher wants his students to watch pre-recorded videos and answer questions embedded in the videos. Which tool would be most suitable for this purpose ?',opts:['A) Edpuzzle','B) Evernote','C) Google Docs','D) Strawpoll'],ans:0,exp:'Only Edpuzzle is designed to combine video playback with embedded, timestamped questions, exactly matching the teacher\'s requirement. Hence Option (A) is correct. Modern Teaching Support Systems include a wide range of ICT tools, each purpose-built for a specific pedagogical function: Edpuzzle – lets a teacher take any video (own recording or from platforms like YouTube), trim it, add voice-over/annotations, and embed formative-assessment…'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_g',no:10,q:'An instructor uses portfolio assessments in an art class. What key components should be included in the students\' portfolios ? (A) Collection of completed art works ; (B) Reflective essays on their creative process ; (C) Standardized test scores ; (D) Peer feedback and critiques Choose the correct answer from the options given below :',opts:['A) (B) and (C) Only','B) (A), (B) and (D) Only','C) (A) and (C) Only','D) (C) and (D) Only'],ans:1,exp:'A portfolio in an art class should contain the actual artworks (A), the students\' reflective essays on their creative process (B), and peer feedback/critiques (D) – together these capture product, process, and social/collaborative evaluation. Standardized test scores (C) do not belong, since portfolio assessment is precisely an alternative to standardized, quantitative testing. Hence A, B, D only is correct – Option (B).'},
  // ── 28 Aug 2024 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_f',no:6,q:'Which of the following best exemplifies the zero transfer of learning?',opts:['A) Using knowledge of one programming language to another similar language','B) Applying techniques of learning in basketball to improve skills in soccer','C) Using skills acquired in carpentry to excel in painting','D) Applying a mathematical problem solving method to physics problems'],ans:2,exp:'Options (A), (B) and (D) all describe pairs of tasks that share genuine common elements – logical structure (programming), general athletic/strategic skills (sport-to-sport), or quantitative reasoning (maths-to-physics) – so each represents positive transfer. Carpentry and painting, by contrast, draw on largely unrelated skill sets (manual joinery and construction versus visual-artistic technique), so proficiency in one carries no systematic advantage (or disadvantage) into the other – the defining signature of zero transfer.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_f',no:7,q:'Which of the following channels of Swayamprabha are under the Consortium for Educational Communication (CEC), New Delhi? (A) Channel 11 (B) Channel 1 (C) Channel 15 (D) Channel 3 (E) Channel 6 Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A), (B), (C) and (E) Only','B) (C), (D) and (E) Only','C) (A) and (C) Only','D) (B), (D) and (E) Only'],ans:3,exp:'Among the five listed channels, Channels 1, 3 and 6 are the ones administered by the Consortium for Educational Communication (CEC), New Delhi under the Swayamprabha DTH scheme, while Channels 11 and 15 fall under different nodal agencies. This matches option (D) exactly.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_f',no:8,q:'Match List - I with List - II. <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>2@lList - I (App) List - II (Use)</th></tr><tr><td>(A) Labster</td><td>(I) Real-time polling</td></tr><tr><td>(B) Google Earth</td><td>(II) Virtual labs and simulations</td></tr><tr><td>(C) Kahoot</td><td>(III) Virtual field trips and geographic exploration</td></tr><tr><td>(D) Poll Everywhere</td><td>(IV) Online quizzes and assessments</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','B) (A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)','C) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','D) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)'],ans:2,exp:'Each application has one clearly defined, distinct purpose in an ICT-enabled classroom: Labster for virtual labs, Google Earth for geographic/virtual field trips, Kahoot for quizzes, and Poll Everywhere for real-time polling. This one-to-one correspondence matches option (C) exactly.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_f',no:9,q:'A teacher wants to integrate SWAYAM into the curriculum to enhance learning. Which of the following benefits of SWAYAM should the teacher highlight to the students? (A) Free access to quality educational resources (B) Opportunity for live interactions with instructors (C) Peer-reviewed assignments (D) Self-paced learning Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (B), (C) and (D) Only','B) (A) and (D) Only','C) (D) Only','D) (A) and (B) Only'],ans:1,exp:'SWAYAM\'s promotional and design identity rests on two pillars: it is free and openly accessible, and it lets learners study at their own pace through recorded video lectures and downloadable material. Live instructor interaction and formal peer review are not the platform\'s defining characteristics, so only (A) and (D) should be highlighted – option (B).'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_f',no:10,q:'The reduced ability to remember information acquired after the onset of Amnesia is known as:',opts:['A) Working memory','B) Retrograde amnesia','C) Phonological loop','D) Anterograde amnesia'],ans:3,exp:'By definition, anterograde amnesia is the impaired ability to encode and later recall new information encountered after the onset of the condition – exactly the deficit described in the question – making option (D) correct. Amnesia (memory loss) is classically divided into two types based on the timing of the affected memories relative to the onset of the causal event (injury, disease, trauma): retrograde amnesia is the inability to recall memories formed before onset, while anterograde amnesia is…'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_f',no:40,q:'A SWAYAM course is classified based on the engagement of number of ______ and number of video hours.',opts:['A) semesters','B) months','C) weeks','D) years'],ans:2,exp:'SWAYAM courses are structured week-by-week (a course is commonly described as, e.g., a 12-week course), with an associated number of video-lecture hours per week – this is the standard classification template, matching option (C). SWAYAM courses are structured and classified according to a fixed template: they are organised in week-wise modules (a typical SWAYAM course runs for a defined number of weeks, commonly 12–15 weeks), combined with a specified number of video-lecture hours, rather than being…'},
  // ── 23 Aug 2024 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_d',no:6,q:'Which of the following teaching techniques do not represent modern and ICT based teaching method. A. Recitation and Memorization ; B. Flipped Classroom ; C. Mind maps ; D. Chalk and Talk Method ; E. Gamification Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) B and C Only','B) C and D Only','C) A and D Only','D) D and E Only'],ans:2,exp:'Only A (Recitation and Memorization) and D (Chalk and Talk Method) are purely traditional, teacher-centred, non-technological practices. B, C and E all either directly depend on or are commonly delivered through digital/ICT tools and active-learning design, so they are correctly classified as modern/ICT-based – leaving A and D as the two techniques that do not qualify, matching Option C.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_d',no:7,q:'Match the List-I with List-II [LIST I Concept: A. Academic year ; B. Credit Point ; C. Credit ; D. Course]<br>[LIST II Description: I. A unit by which the course work is measured. It defines the number of hours of instruction required per week ; II. Papers taught under the programme duly defining the learning objectives and outcomes ; III. Two consecutive (one odd and one even) semesters ; IV. The product of grade point and number of credits for a course] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I','B) A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II','C) A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II','D) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I'],ans:2,exp:'Each CBCS term has one precise UGC definition: Academic Year is a time-block (two semesters), Credit is a workload unit (hours/week), Course is a curricular unit (a paper with objectives/outcomes), and Credit Point is a computed quantity (grade point $\\times$ credits) used purely for GPA calculation. Mapping A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II respects all four definitions simultaneously, and only Option C reproduces this exact combination.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_d',no:8,q:'Match the List-I with List-II [LIST I Jackson\'s learning style: A. High sensation seeker ; B. Goal oriented achievers ; C. Emotionally Intelligent Achiever ; D. Conscientious Achiever]<br>[LIST II Description: I. Responsible, rational learners, endowed with an urge to explore the world so that positive goals can be achieved ; II. Learns from their mistakes, possess a scientific, detached and autonomous learning style, processes information in an emotionally intelligent manner ; III. Gets involved in new challenging activities ; IV. One who sets learning goals which are generally hard and specific and has confidence to achieve the goals] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV','B) A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I','C) A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II','D) A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II'],ans:1,exp:'Three of the four matches can be located almost by keyword alone: “challenging activities” signals sensation-seeking (A-III); “sets learning goals... hard and specific” signals goal-orientation (B-IV); and “emotionally intelligent manner” directly names the Emotionally Intelligent Achiever (C-II). By elimination, the remaining description (“responsible, rational learners... urge to explore”) must belong to the Conscientious Achiever (D-I). This full mapping is reproduced only by Option B.'},
  // ── 23 Aug 2024 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_c',no:6,q:'What among the following can be correctly claimed in the light of ‘Assessment Rubrics\'? (A) A tool used to interpret and grade students on the basis of some criterion. ; (B) A means to increase subjective evaluation by the evaluator. ; (C) It ensures transparency and fairness in the marking process. ; (D) It provides an opportunity to set orbitrary standards and guidelines for moderation. ; (E) It is an innovative evaluation system. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A), (C), and (D) only','B) (A), (B) and (C) only','C) (C), (D), and (E) only','D) (A), (C), and (E) only'],ans:3,exp:'An Assessment Rubric is a scoring guide that lists explicit, pre-defined criteria and performance levels used to evaluate student work consistently. Its entire purpose is to reduce subjectivity and arbitrariness by making the criteria for each grade transparent to both the evaluator and the learner, thereby improving fairness, consistency (inter-rater reliability), and transparency of the marking process.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_c',no:7,q:'Match List - I with List - II. <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>List - I (Types of Teaching)</th><th>List - II (Description)</th></tr><tr><td>(A) Macro teaching</td><td>(I) A teaching technique especially used in teacher\'s pre-service education to train them systematically</td></tr><tr><td>(B) Micro teaching</td><td>(II) An approach where the educators divide the content among themselves and students sit with one educator before moving to the other</td></tr><tr><td>(C) Station teaching</td><td>(III) A situation in a classroom where two teachers work on a class together</td></tr><tr><td>(D) Cooperative teaching</td><td>(IV) When a teacher provides instruction to the entire class at one time for an extended period of time</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','B) (A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)','C) (A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)','D) (A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)'],ans:2,exp:'Macro teaching is ordinary, whole-class instruction delivered by one teacher for an extended period. Micro teaching is a controlled, scaled-down practice technique used mainly in pre-service teacher training. Station teaching divides content/students among teachers who rotate. Cooperative (co-)teaching has two teachers jointly responsible for the same class at the same time.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_c',no:8,q:'What among the following can be ascribed to learning styles? (A) It is a way ‘how\' the student learns ; (B) They are strengths and preferences of the learners for responding to the stimuli in the environment ; (C) It is ‘What\' the learner learns ; (D) It is a behavioural pattern developed for any new learning Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A) and (B) only','B) (A) and (C) only','C) (A), (B), and (D) only','D) (B), (C), and (D) only'],ans:2,exp:'Learning styles refer to the characteristic, preferred manner in which an individual perceives, processes, and responds to learning stimuli (e.g. visual, auditory, kinaesthetic preferences) – i.e. how a learner learns, not what is learnt (that is the domain of curriculum/content).'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_c',no:9,q:'What can be correctly inferred about the Traditional Teaching Method and Support System? (A) Teacher is responsible to control the class and teach with the usage of blackboard ; (B) It is also known as ‘Back-to-Basics\' System. ; (C) The student carries the responsibilities to use self-learning tools. ; (D) Virtual labs are used as one of the Teaching tools. ; (E) Charts, Maps and Textbooks are the prime resources and handouts are used by the teacher Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (B) and (C) only','B) (A), (B) and (D) only','C) (D) and (E) only','D) (A), (B), and (E) only'],ans:3,exp:'The Traditional Teaching Method is teacher-centred: the teacher controls the class, delivers content using the blackboard and static print resources (charts, maps, textbooks, handouts), and is also called the "Back-to-Basics" System. Self-directed learning tools and technology-based tools such as virtual labs belong to modern/ICT-based, learner-centred methods, not the traditional method.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_c',no:10,q:'What among the following is incorrect about SWAYAM Prabha?',opts:['A) It was launched by Sri Abul Kalam Azad.','B) Satellite is used for telecasting the educational contents through DTH channels.','C) The web portal of SWAYAM Prabha is maintained by INFLIBNET, Gujarat.','D) The contents for SWAYAM Prabha are provided by NPTEL, IITs, UGC, CEC and IGNOU.'],ans:0,exp:'SWAYAM Prabha is a group of 32 DTH (Direct-To-Home) channels devoted to telecasting high-quality educational programmes on a 24x7 basis using satellite communication. It is one of India\'s flagship digital-initiative programmes in higher education (mentioned explicitly in the UGC NET Paper I syllabus under "Methods of teaching ... Off-line vs. On-line methods").'},
  // ── 22 Aug 2024 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_e',no:6,q:'‘I never teach my pupils; I only attempt to provide the conditions in which they can learn\' is a statement by :',opts:['A) Swami Vivekananda','B) Albert Einstein','C) Sri Aurbindo','D) Aristotle'],ans:1,exp:'This exact quotation – emphasising that a teacher\'s true role is to create favourable learning conditions rather than to directly ‘teach\' – is attributed to Albert Einstein, making Option (B) correct. This is a direct recall-based question on the philosophy of teaching, specifically the facilitative/constructivist view of the teacher\'s role – the idea that a teacher\'s job is not to ‘transmit\' knowledge but to create the right environment/conditions under which learning…'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_e',no:7,q:'In the context of learning, which of the following DOES NOT represent the self concept?',opts:['A) It is what an individual feels about others.','B) It is what an individual feels how others think/perceive of him/her.','C) It is an awareness of the individual about his/her strengths.','D) It is an awareness of the individual about his/her weaknesses.'],ans:0,exp:'Self concept is about how a person perceives and evaluates themselves (their strengths, weaknesses, and their sense of how others view them). Feeling something about other people is not part of one\'s self concept, so Option (A) is the statement that does NOT represent it.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_e',no:8,q:'Which of the following correctly represent characteristics of teaching ? (A) To provide guidance and training to students ; (B) Interaction between authorities ; (C) To provide formal and informal education ; (D) An art to import knowledge to students in an effective way ; (E) To prepare policies for corporate bodies Choose the correct answer from the options given below :',opts:['A) (A) and (B) Only','B) (A), (D) and (E) Only','C) (A), (C) and (D) Only','D) (C), (D) and (E) Only'],ans:2,exp:'Teaching genuinely involves guiding/training students (A), operating through both formal and informal channels (C), and being the art of imparting knowledge effectively (D). It does not involve inter-authority interaction (B) or drafting corporate policy (E), so the correct combination is A, C, D – Option (C).'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_e',no:9,q:'Match List - I with List - II. [List - I: A. Converger ; B. Diverger ; C. Assimilator ; D. Accomodator]<br>[List - II: I. They are best at concrete experience and reflective observation. Their strength is their imaginative ability. They tend to be interested in people and emotional elements. ; II. They are good at Abstract Conceptualization and Active Experimentation like solving problems; practical application of ideas. ; III. They enjoy and seek new experiences and adapt to new situations; and are intuitive, risk-takers, open minded. ; IV. Ability to create theoretical models; possess sound logic, inductive reasoning, Analytic less interested in people.] Choose the correct answer from the options given below :',opts:['A) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','B) (A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','C) (A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)','D) (A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)'],ans:2,exp:'Applying Kolb\'s model precisely: Converger\'s defining trait is practical, active-experimentation problem-solving (II); Diverger is imaginative and reflective, strong on concrete experience (I); Assimilator is theoretical/logical and less people-oriented (IV); Accommodator is adaptive, intuitive and a risk-taker who seeks new experiences (III). This gives A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III – Option (C).'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_e',no:10,q:'“Education is the manifestation of perfection already in man” is a statement by :',opts:['A) Rabindranath Tagore','B) M.K. Gandhi','C) Sri Aurobindo','D) Swami Vivekananda'],ans:3,exp:'“Education is the manifestation of perfection already in man” is one of Swami Vivekananda\'s most famous and frequently quoted definitions of education, reflecting his view that education draws out innate perfection rather than adding external information – hence Option (D) is correct.'},
  // ── 21 Aug 2024 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_b',no:6,q:'Reena wanted to eat an ice-cream. She took out a phone number online. She repeated the number over and over again while dialing. After order was delivered she forgot the number. This is an example of the use of which memory process?',opts:['A) Short-term memory','B) Automatic Processing','C) Echoic Memory','D) Iconic Memory'],ans:0,exp:'Reena\'s behaviour – repeating the number to keep it active only as long as needed, then losing it – is the defining signature of short-term/working memory, which requires continuous rehearsal for retention and fades quickly once that rehearsal stops. This question falls under Unit I: Teaching Aptitude – Learner\'s characteristics (cognitive processes). Human memory is broadly classified into sensory memory, short-term (working) memory, and long-term memory.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_b',no:7,q:'The contents of Swayamprabha channel –4 with the theme of “Education and Home Science” are provided by:',opts:['A) CEC, New Delhi','B) IIT, Madras','C) IGNOU, New Delhi','D) IIT Delhi'],ans:0,exp:'Swayamprabha Channel–4 is designated for general/higher education and home science content, an area under the mandate of the Consortium for Educational Communication (CEC), New Delhi, which has historically produced UGC countrywide classroom/educational programming.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_b',no:8,q:'Which higher-order knowledge can make the difference between how well and quickly one\'s students learn material?',opts:['A) Declarative','B) Rote','C) Metacognition','D) Procedural'],ans:2,exp:'Metacognitive knowledge allows learners to plan how to approach a learning task, monitor their comprehension while learning, and evaluate their progress afterward – this self-regulatory control is precisely what differentiates efficient, fast learners from inefficient ones, independent of the raw facts or procedures being learned.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_b',no:9,q:'Which of the following are the interactive whiteboard softwares that allow teachers to create digital lessons, annotate documents and collaborate with students in real time? A. Jamboard B. Camtasia C. Stormboard D. Concept board E. Plotagon Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) B and C only','B) A, C and D only','C) C, D and E only','D) A, B and E only'],ans:1,exp:'Jamboard, Stormboard, and Concept board are all purpose-built, real-time collaborative digital-whiteboard applications used for creating and annotating lessons together with students, while Camtasia (video recording/editing) and Plotagon (animated video creation) serve entirely different functions.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_b',no:10,q:'In which type of assessment do students measure their own progress against their previous performance rather than against external criteria or peers?',opts:['A) Peer Assessment','B) Ipsative Assessment','C) Formative Assessment','D) Summative Assessment'],ans:1,exp:'The term “ipsative” derives from the Latin ipse (“self”), and ipsative assessment is explicitly defined as measuring a student\'s growth relative to their own prior results, which is exactly the scenario described in the question. This question falls under Unit I: Teaching Aptitude – Evaluation Systems. Ipsative assessment compares a learner\'s current performance against their own earlier performance (self-referenced), rather than against an external standard (criterion-referenced) or against other…'},
  // ── 21 Aug 2024 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_a',no:5,q:'The number of digits an individual can report back without error in a single presentation represents',opts:['A) chunking','B) memory span','C) phonological loop','D) Time Zone'],ans:1,exp:'The definition given in the question is the textbook definition of memory span – the number of digits (or other items) an individual can accurately reproduce immediately after a single exposure. None of the other three options describe a numerical count of correctly recalled items, so Option B is the only precise match.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_a',no:6,q:'A history teacher wants to create an interactive timeline showcasing the evolution of different art movements over centuries? Which tool would best support the creation of this multimedia presentation?',opts:['A) Prezi','B) Adobe Illustrator','C) Flipgrid','D) Google forms'],ans:0,exp:'Prezi is the only tool among the four that is specifically designed to create interactive, non-linear, timeline-style multimedia presentations, making it the natural choice for showcasing the evolution of art movements across centuries; the other three tools serve entirely different instructional functions (static graphic design, video discussion, and form-based data collection respectively).'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_a',no:7,q:'A collection of the student\'s work in an area, showing growth, self reflection, and achievements is known as:-',opts:['A) Modelling','B) Portfolio','C) Mastery goal','D) Problem solving'],ans:1,exp:'The definition given – a collection of a student\'s work in an area, showing growth, self-reflection, and achievement – is the standard, textbook definition of a portfolio in educational assessment, distinguishing it clearly from the other three options, none of which refer to a compiled body of student work.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_a',no:8,q:'Match the List-I with List-II [LIST I: A. Channel 03 (PRABODH) ; B. Channel 04 (SAARASWAT) ; C. Channel 05 (PRABANDHAN) ; D. Channel 06 (VIDHIK)]<br>[LIST II: I. Education and Home Science ; II. Information, Communication and Management Studies ; III. Law and Legal Studies ; IV. Social and Behavioural Sciences] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I','B) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','C) A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III','D) A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV'],ans:2,exp:'Each Swayamprabha channel name is drawn from a Hindi/Sanskrit root that directly signals its subject theme – Prabodh (awareness) for Social and Behavioural Sciences, Saaraswat (learning) for Education and Home Science, Prabandhan (management) for ICT and Management Studies, and Vidhik (legal) for Law – giving the matching A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III, which is Option C.'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'aug24_a',no:9,q:'Match the List-I with List-II [LIST I: A. Decision making ; B. Confirmation bias ; C. Belief perseverance ; D. Overconfidence bias]<br>[LIST II: I. The tendency to hold on a belief in the face of contradictory evidence. ; II. The tendency to have more confidence in judgement and decisions than we should based on probability. ; III. The tendency to search for and use information that supports our ideas rather than refuses them. ; IV. Evaluating alternatives and making choices among them.] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I','B) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I','C) A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II','D) A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III'],ans:2,exp:'Each of the four terms has a precise, non-overlapping textbook definition: decision making is the general act of choosing among alternatives (IV), confirmation bias is selectively searching for supporting evidence (III), belief perseverance is refusing to update a belief despite contrary evidence (I), and overconfidence bias is unwarranted excess confidence in one\'s judgements (II) – giving the matching A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II, which is Option C.'},
  // ── 11 Dec 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_e',no:6,q:'Which of the following is not a benefit of Mind Mapping?',opts:['A) Improving memory retention','B) Enhancing creativity and problem solving skills','C) Reducing stress and anxiety','D) Making information more difficult to comprehend'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Mind Mapping</b> is a visual, non-linear note-making technique developed by Tony Buzan in which a central idea is placed in the middle of a page and related sub-ideas branch outward, usually supported by colours, images, and keywords. Because it mirrors the brain’s associative (radiant) thinking pattern, it is treated in the UGC NET syllabus as a modern, learner-centred instructional/support technique that improves comprehension rather than hindering it.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 – Given information:</b> Four statements about the effects of Mind Mapping are given; three describe genuine, well-documented benefits and one describes an effect opposite to its purpose.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 – Required result:</b> Identify the statement that is <i>not</i> a benefit of Mind Mapping.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 – Principle used:</b> Mind Mapping externalises and organises thought visually, which by definition simplifies comprehension of complex material – it cannot logically make information harder to understand.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 – Evaluate each statement against the principle:</b> Improved memory retention (visual/spatial encoding aids recall), enhanced creativity and problem solving (non-linear branching encourages associative thinking), and reduced stress/anxiety (organising a topic reduces cognitive overload) are all established, syllabus-recognised benefits.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 – Isolate the contradictory statement:</b> “Making information more difficult to comprehend” directly contradicts the core purpose of the technique – simplification and clarity.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 – Conclusion:</b> Since Options 1, 2 and 3 are true benefits, only Option 4 remains as the statement that is <i>not</i> a benefit.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 – Correct option:</b> Option 4.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Mind Mapping is explicitly designed to make complex information <i>easier</i>, not harder, to grasp – by breaking a topic into a visual hierarchy of branches and keywords, it reduces the cognitive load of processing dense text. Option 4 states the reverse of this purpose and is therefore the correct choice for “not a benefit.”</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Not the answer)</b></span> – Improving memory retention is a genuine, well-documented benefit of Mind Mapping (visual-spatial encoding strengthens recall); students sometimes wrongly pick this because they underestimate how strongly visual techniques aid memory.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Not the answer)</b></span> – Enhancing creativity and problem-solving is a core, intended benefit, since branching structures encourage lateral/associative connections between ideas.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Not the answer)</b></span> – Reducing stress and anxiety is a recognised secondary benefit, as organising scattered information into a clear structure lowers the learner’s cognitive burden.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Making information <i>more difficult</i> to comprehend is the opposite of what Mind Mapping achieves, making this the one statement that is not a benefit.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Mind Mapping is a visual, radiant-thinking note-making technique (Tony Buzan).</li><li>Genuine benefits: better memory retention, enhanced creativity/problem-solving, reduced stress, easier comprehension.</li><li>NTA frequently tests this topic using an “identify the exception/NOT a benefit” format – always look for the option that contradicts the tool’s basic purpose.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_e',no:7,q:'Which of the following are not the characteristics of Adolescent learners? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> They have uniformity in their physical development.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> They have stable self-concepts.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> They often experience mood swings and emotional turmoil.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> They have high hopes and aspirations for their future.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">E.</span> They have increased peer group pressure.</li></ol> Choose the <i>correct</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, C, D and E only','B) C and D only','C) B, C and E only','D) A and B only'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Adolescence (roughly ages 13–19) is a transitional developmental stage marked by rapid but <i>highly variable</i> physical growth (not uniform across individuals), an emerging and often <i>unstable</i> self-identity, intense emotional fluctuation, strong future-orientation/aspiration, and heightened sensitivity to peer approval. The UGC NET syllabus lists learner characteristics under Academic, Social, Emotional and Cognitive domains, and adolescence is the single most-tested developmental stage.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 – Given information:</b> Five statements (A–E) about adolescent learners are given; the question asks which are <i>not</i> true characteristics.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 – Required result:</b> Identify exactly which statements misdescribe adolescents.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 – Principle used:</b> Compare each statement against the well-established developmental-psychology profile of adolescence (physical variability, identity instability, emotional turbulence, aspiration, peer pressure).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 – Evaluate Statement A:</b> “Uniformity in physical development” is <b>false</b> – adolescence is characterised by highly <i>uneven</i>, individual-paced growth spurts (the opposite of uniformity).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 – Evaluate Statement B:</b> “Stable self-concepts” is <b>false</b> – adolescents are actively forming their identity (Erikson’s identity-vs-role-confusion stage), so their self-concept is typically unstable, not stable.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 – Evaluate Statements C, D, E:</b> Mood swings/emotional turmoil (C), high future aspirations (D), and increased peer pressure (E) are all textbook-accurate, well-documented adolescent traits – hence <b>true</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 – Correct option:</b> Only A and B are <i>not</i> genuine characteristics, so the answer is Option 4.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The question is a negative (“NOT”) statement question, so the two developmentally <i>inaccurate</i> descriptions – claiming adolescents have uniform physical growth and stable self-concepts – are exactly the two that must be selected. All the remaining statements (C, D, E) match the accepted profile of adolescent learners and are therefore excluded from the answer.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – A, C, D and E only – wrongly treats A as false (correct) but also wrongly includes C, D and E, which are true characteristics, not false ones.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – C and D only – both C and D are genuinely true characteristics of adolescents, so neither belongs in a “NOT a characteristic” list.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – B, C and E only – correctly includes B but wrongly adds C and E, which are true adolescent traits.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – A and B only – correctly isolates the two statements (uniform physical development, stable self-concept) that misrepresent adolescence.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Adolescents show <i>uneven</i>, not uniform, physical growth.</li><li>Adolescents have an <i>unstable</i>, forming self-concept (identity formation stage), not a stable one.</li><li>True traits: mood swings/emotional turmoil, high aspirations, strong peer-group influence.</li><li>Watch for “NOT/not a characteristic” framing – always test each statement against the real developmental profile before selecting.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_e',no:8,q:'Which of the following are the National coordinators for the production of best quality content in SWAYAM? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> NCTE</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> AICTE</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> IGNOU</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> UGC</li><li><span class="p1-lab">E.</span> CEC</li></ol> Choose the <i>correct</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A), (B), (D) and (E) only.','B) (B), (C), (D) and (E) only.','C) (B) and (C) only','D) (A) and (D) only.'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>SWAYAM</b> (Study Webs of Active Learning for Young Aspiring Minds) is India’s national MOOC platform (an “On-line vs Off-line teaching method” explicitly named in the Unit-I syllabus). To ensure quality, the Ministry of Education designated <b>nine National Coordinators</b>, each responsible for a specific education segment: <b>AICTE</b> (self-paced &amp; international courses), <b>NPTEL</b> (engineering), <b>UGC</b> (non-technical postgraduate education), <b>CEC</b> (undergraduate education), <b>NCERT</b> and <b>NIOS</b> (school education), <b>IGNOU</b> (out-of-school students), <b>IIMB</b> (management studies), and <b>NITTTR</b> (teacher-training programmes). <b>NCTE</b> (the teacher-education regulator) is <i>not</i> one of the nine designated coordinators.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 – Given information:</b> Five bodies (NCTE, AICTE, IGNOU, UGC, CEC) are listed as candidates for being SWAYAM National Coordinators.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 – Required result:</b> Identify exactly which of these five are among the official National Coordinators of SWAYAM.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 – Principle used:</b> Recall the officially notified list of nine SWAYAM National Coordinators (as released by the Ministry of Education/UGC).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 – Check each body:</b> AICTE – yes, coordinator for self-paced/international courses. IGNOU – yes, coordinator for out-of-school learners. UGC – yes, coordinator for non-technical PG education. CEC – yes, coordinator for undergraduate education.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 – Check NCTE:</b> NCTE (National Council for Teacher Education) is a teacher-education regulatory body, but it is <i>not</i> on the notified list of nine SWAYAM coordinators – that role for school/teacher-related content is instead held by NCERT, NIOS and NITTTR.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 – Combine results:</b> The correct set of coordinators among the given five is B (AICTE), C (IGNOU), D (UGC), E (CEC); A (NCTE) is excluded.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 – Correct option:</b> Option 2 – (B), (C), (D) and (E) only.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Four of the five listed bodies – AICTE, IGNOU, UGC and CEC – are genuinely part of the officially notified panel of nine National Coordinators for SWAYAM. NCTE, despite being an important education regulator, does not appear on this specific list, so any option including A is incorrect, making Option 2 the only fully accurate combination.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – (A), (B), (D) and (E) only – wrongly includes NCTE (A) and wrongly excludes IGNOU (C), both of which are reversed from the correct list.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – (B), (C), (D) and (E) only – correctly lists AICTE, IGNOU, UGC and CEC as the four genuine coordinators among the five given bodies.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – (B) and (C) only – correctly includes AICTE and IGNOU but wrongly omits UGC and CEC, which are also genuine coordinators.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – (A) and (D) only – wrongly includes NCTE (A), which is not a SWAYAM coordinator at all.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>SWAYAM has <b>nine</b> National Coordinators: AICTE, NPTEL, UGC, CEC, NCERT, NIOS, IGNOU, IIMB, NITTTR.</li><li>NCTE is <i>not</i> on this list – a favourite NTA distractor.</li><li>Quick memory link: UGC $\\to$ PG (non-technical); CEC $\\to$ UG; AICTE $\\to$ self-paced/international; IGNOU $\\to$ out-of-school learners.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_e',no:9,q:'Which of the following is not the objective of teaching?',opts:['A) To help students to become better thinkers','B) To facilitate the development of problem solving ability among students.','C) To follow a rigid teaching plan','D) To help learner, adjust his perceptions to reality, attaining harmony between internal and external experiences'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">The <b>objectives of teaching</b>, as understood in the UGC NET Teaching Aptitude unit, centre on developing the learner’s thinking, reasoning, problem-solving and self-understanding abilities, and on helping the learner adjust meaningfully to their environment. Teaching is meant to be <b>flexible and learner-responsive</b>, adapting the plan to the learners’ needs – rigidity in following a fixed plan works against effective teaching-learning, since it ignores individual differences and classroom feedback.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 – Given information:</b> Four statements describe possible objectives of teaching.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 – Required result:</b> Find the one statement that does <i>not</i> represent a genuine objective of teaching.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 – Principle used:</b> Genuine objectives of teaching are learner-centred and developmental (thinking skills, problem-solving, perceptual/emotional harmony); rigidity in method is a pedagogical limitation, not an objective.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 – Evaluate Option 1:</b> Helping students become better thinkers is a core, universally accepted teaching objective – <b>true objective</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 – Evaluate Option 2:</b> Facilitating problem-solving ability is likewise a standard, syllabus-recognised objective – <b>true objective</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 – Evaluate Options 3 and 4:</b> “Following a rigid teaching plan” describes an inflexible <i>method</i>, not a learning outcome, and actually contradicts good teaching practice (which requires adapting to learners); “helping the learner adjust perceptions to reality” is a recognised humanistic objective of teaching (aligned with self-actualisation/adjustment theories) – <b>true objective</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 – Correct option:</b> Only Option 3 fails to describe a genuine objective, so Option 3 is correct.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Effective teaching objectives focus on what the <i>learner</i> should gain – better thinking, problem-solving skills, and a realistic, harmonious self-world adjustment. “Following a rigid teaching plan” describes teacher-centred inflexibility, which is the opposite of good teaching practice, making it the correct choice for the objective that does <i>not</i> belong.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Not the answer)</b></span> – Developing better thinkers is an authentic teaching objective connected directly to cognitive development.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Not the answer)</b></span> – Facilitating problem-solving ability is a standard, widely cited teaching objective.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Rigidity in following a teaching plan is a methodological flaw, not a learner-oriented objective; good teachers adapt their plan to classroom needs.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Not the answer)</b></span> – Helping learners reconcile internal perceptions with external reality is a recognised humanistic/psychological objective of teaching.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Teaching objectives are learner-centred: better thinking, problem-solving, perceptual-emotional adjustment/harmony.</li><li>Rigidity of method is a limitation, never an objective, of good teaching.</li><li>NTA often tests this unit with a single “odd one out” MCQ rather than a multi-statement combination question.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_e',no:10,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> Statement (I): Edpuzzle is a tool used for mind mapping in education.<br> Statement (II): Mindmeister is a tool used for creating interactive videos in education.<br> In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are true.','B) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are false.','C) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.','D) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Under <b>ICT-based Teaching Support Systems</b>, two specific tools are commonly tested: <b>Edpuzzle</b> is an ed-tech platform for turning existing videos into <i>interactive video lessons</i> (embedding questions, voice-overs and checkpoints); <b>MindMeister</b> is a <i>mind-mapping and brainstorming</i> tool for creating visual, branching concept maps online. The two statements in this question have deliberately <b>swapped</b> the correct function of each tool.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 – Given information:</b> Statement I claims Edpuzzle is a mind-mapping tool; Statement II claims MindMeister is an interactive-video tool.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 – Required result:</b> Determine the truth value of each statement independently.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 – Principle used:</b> Match each tool to its actual, well-documented ed-tech function.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 – Evaluate Statement I:</b> Edpuzzle’s actual function is creating <i>interactive video lessons</i>, not mind maps – so Statement I is <b>false</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 – Evaluate Statement II:</b> MindMeister’s actual function is <i>mind mapping</i>, not interactive video creation – so Statement II is also <b>false</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 – Combine results:</b> Both statements describe the tools’ functions in reverse of reality, so both are false.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 – Correct option:</b> Option 2 – Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are false.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The question tests precise factual recognition of two specific ed-tech tools by deliberately interchanging their real-world functions. Since Edpuzzle is actually for interactive videos (not mind mapping) and MindMeister is actually for mind mapping (not interactive videos), both given statements are factually incorrect, making Option 2 the correct choice.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Both true – incorrect, since both tool-function pairings given in the question are reversed from reality.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Both false – correctly identifies that both statements swap the tools’ real functions.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Statement I true, II false – incorrect because Statement I (Edpuzzle = mind mapping) is also false, not true.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Statement I false, II true – correctly marks Statement I as false but wrongly marks Statement II as true, when it is also false.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Edpuzzle</b> = interactive video-lesson tool (embeds questions into videos).</li><li><b>MindMeister</b> = online mind-mapping/brainstorming tool.</li><li>NTA frequently swaps tool-function pairs in two-statement questions – always verify each tool’s function independently rather than assuming symmetry.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 08 Dec 2023 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_d',no:6,q:'A teacher’s belief that he or she can reach even the most difficult students and help them learn is called',opts:['A) Teacher’s knowledge','B) Teacher’s understanding','C) Teacher’s sense of efficacy','D) Teacher’s self-perception'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Concept required:</b> <i>Teacher self-efficacy</i> (rooted in Bandura’s social-cognitive theory of self-efficacy) is a teacher’s own judgment of their capability to organise and execute the courses of action needed to bring about desired learning outcomes, <i>even in difficult students</i>. It is distinct from mere subject “knowledge” or generic “self-perception”.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> The statement describes a specific <i>belief</i> (not knowledge, not a general self-image) about one’s own capability to positively impact even the most difficult learners.</li><li><b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Identify the standard educational-psychology term for this specific belief.</li><li><b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Bandura’s self-efficacy theory, applied to teaching, defines “teacher efficacy” precisely as a teacher’s belief in their own capacity to affect student learning, regardless of a student’s difficulty level or background.</li><li><b>Step 4 (Match to options):</b> “Knowledge” (Option 1) is about subject-matter mastery, not belief in impact. “Understanding” (Option 2) is a cognitive grasp of content/learners, not a confidence belief. “Self-perception” (Option 4) is a broader, vaguer self-image construct not specific to instructional impact.</li><li><b>Step 5–7 (Conclusion):</b> Only “teacher’s sense of efficacy” precisely names this belief construct – <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Conceptual explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Teacher efficacy has two commonly cited dimensions in educational psychology: (a) general teaching efficacy – belief that teaching <i>in general</i> can overcome factors like poor home environment, and (b) personal teaching efficacy – belief in <i>one’s own</i> skill to teach effectively. The question statement (“he or she can reach even the most difficult students”) squarely matches this construct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Explanation of every option &amp; why the others are incorrect</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — Teacher’s knowledge – refers to command over subject matter or pedagogy, not a belief about impact on difficult learners; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — Teacher’s understanding – refers to comprehension of content or learners, a cognitive rather than a confidence/belief construct; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> — Teacher’s sense of efficacy – the precise, standard term for a teacher’s belief in their own capability to help even difficult students learn; correct.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — Teacher’s self-perception – too broad and generic (covers self-image on many dimensions, not specifically instructional-impact belief); incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Justification of the correct answer</div><div class="p1x-b">The question’s wording is a near-verbatim paraphrase of the standard textbook definition of teacher (self-)efficacy, making Option 3 the only semantically exact match.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Alternative solution method</div><div class="p1x-b">Keyword-matching approach: the phrase “belief that he/she <i>can</i>” is a classic efficacy-belief marker (compare Bandura’s self-efficacy = “belief in one’s capabilities”); whenever a UGC NET stem uses “belief that I/the teacher can...”, efficacy is almost always the intended answer.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Teacher (self-)efficacy = belief in one’s own capability to help even difficult students learn.</li><li>Rooted in Bandura’s self-efficacy theory, applied to the teaching profession.</li><li>Distinguish it from “knowledge” (subject mastery) and “self-perception” (broad self-image).</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>“I CAN teach” = Efficacy.</b> Any statement built around the word “can” (I can reach them, I can help them learn) signals efficacy; a statement about “knowing” signals knowledge; a statement about “seeing myself as...” signals self-perception.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Confusing “self-efficacy” with the more general “self-perception” or “self-concept” – efficacy is always task/outcome specific (can I achieve X?), while self-perception/self-concept is a broader, trait-like self-image.</div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_d',no:7,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> Flip (ICT tool) allows users to record and share short video responses.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> Flip (ICT tool) is primarily used for video discussions and presentations.<br> In light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate</i> answer from the options given below',opts:['A) Both statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect','D) statement I incorrect but statement II is correct'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Concept required:</b> Flip (rebranded from “Flipgrid”) is a free Microsoft-owned educational video-discussion platform. Its core function is exactly as described in both statements: students/teachers record and share short video responses (Statement I), and the platform is used broadly for video-based discussions and presentations in a classroom or asynchronous setting (Statement II).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> Statement I describes Flip’s core recording/sharing function; Statement II describes its primary use-case.</li><li><b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Determine the truth value of each statement independently.</li><li><b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Match each statement against the documented functionality of the Flip platform.</li><li><b>Step 4 (Evaluate Statement I):</b> Flip’s defining feature is precisely short video recording and sharing among a class/group – <b>True</b>.</li><li><b>Step 5 (Evaluate Statement II):</b> Flip is indeed used for structured video discussions, reflections and presentations in both live and asynchronous teaching – <b>True</b>.</li><li><b>Step 6 (Combine):</b> Both statements are factually accurate descriptions of the same tool, and are complementary rather than contradictory.</li><li><b>Step 7 (Correct option):</b> Both correct matches <b>Option 1</b>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Conceptual explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Statement-pair questions on ICT tools require precise recall of what a specific named tool does. Flip is not a generic video-conferencing tool (like Zoom) nor a document tool; its specific niche is short-form, asynchronous or synchronous video responses used for discussion and light presentation work, which both given statements correctly capture from two complementary angles (mechanism vs. use-case).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Explanation of every option &amp; why the others are incorrect</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> — both correct – accurately reflects that Flip both enables short video recording/sharing and is used for video discussions/presentations; correct.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — both incorrect – wrongly rejects two accurate descriptions of the tool; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — I correct, II incorrect – wrongly treats the tool’s use-case (discussions/presentations) as false when it is, in fact, exactly what Flip is used for; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — I incorrect, II correct – wrongly treats the core recording/sharing mechanism as false, when this is Flip’s defining feature; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Justification of the correct answer</div><div class="p1x-b">Since Statement I (mechanism) and Statement II (use-case) both correctly and non-contradictorily describe the same tool from different angles, there is no basis to reject either – Option 1 is the only internally consistent choice.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Alternative solution method</div><div class="p1x-b">Elimination by internal consistency: if a candidate is unsure of the exact tool but notices that both statements describe compatible, non-conflicting facts about the same object (a mechanism and its typical use), “both correct” is statistically the safer choice among NTA’s typical two-statement item design, unless a statement contains an obviously wrong technical claim.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Flip (formerly Flipgrid) = short video recording/sharing platform for classroom discussion.</li><li>Statement I (mechanism) and Statement II (use-case) are both accurate and complementary.</li><li>Answer: both statements correct.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Exam tip</div><div class="p1x-b">For “named ICT tool” statement-pair questions, keep a short personal glossary of tools (Padlet, Flip, Kahoot, Mentimeter, Nearpod, Google Classroom) with one mechanism-line and one use-case-line each – this question format repeats often.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Assuming any tool that involves “video” must be a live video-conferencing tool like Zoom or Google Meet – Flip is specifically an asynchronous/short-form video <i>discussion</i> tool, not a live meeting platform.</div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_d',no:8,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> In Jigsaw, each student in a group is expected to specialize in one aspect of learning task.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> The major focus of the group investigation strategy is on the development of inquiry skills.<br> In light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate</i> answer from the options given below',opts:['A) Both statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect','D) statement I incorrect but statement II is correct'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Concept required:</b> <b>Jigsaw</b> (Aronson) splits a learning task into sub-parts; each group member becomes the “expert” on one sub-part (specialisation) and then teaches it back to the home group. <b>Group Investigation</b> (Sharan &amp; Sharan) is an open-ended cooperative method centred on students planning, investigating and synthesising information – its major goal is the development of <i>inquiry</i> and research skills.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> Statement I concerns Jigsaw’s structural feature (specialisation); Statement II concerns Group Investigation’s pedagogical focus (inquiry skills).</li><li><b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Evaluate the truth of each statement against the standard definitions of these two named strategies.</li><li><b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Recall each method’s defining characteristic from cooperative-learning theory.</li><li><b>Step 4 (Evaluate Statement I):</b> In Jigsaw, the entire point of dividing material is so that each member becomes responsible for (specialises in) one piece before re-teaching it – <b>True</b>.</li><li><b>Step 5 (Evaluate Statement II):</b> Group Investigation is explicitly designed around student-led inquiry: choosing sub-topics, planning investigation, gathering and synthesising information – <b>True</b>.</li><li><b>Step 6 (Combine):</b> Both statements correctly and independently describe their respective strategies.</li><li><b>Step 7 (Correct option):</b> Both correct matches <b>Option 1</b>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Conceptual explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Cooperative learning methods differ chiefly in <i>how</i> they structure interdependence among group members. Jigsaw creates interdependence through <i>division of content</i> (each member is the sole holder of one piece of the puzzle), whereas Group Investigation creates interdependence through <i>shared inquiry process</i> (the group collectively plans and executes an investigation). Both statements target exactly these correct, distinguishing features.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Explanation of every option &amp; why the others are incorrect</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> — both correct – Jigsaw’s specialisation feature and Group Investigation’s inquiry focus are both textbook-accurate; correct.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — both incorrect – wrongly rejects two accurate, standard descriptions; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — I correct, II incorrect – wrongly treats Group Investigation’s well-established inquiry focus as false; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — I incorrect, II correct – wrongly treats Jigsaw’s specialisation feature (its single most defining trait) as false; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Justification of the correct answer</div><div class="p1x-b">Both statements are drawn directly from the standard definitions of these two distinct, well-documented cooperative-learning strategies, with no internal contradiction – Option 1 is fully supported.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Alternative solution method</div><div class="p1x-b">Keyword-anchor method: “specialize in one aspect” is the textbook signature phrase for Jigsaw; “inquiry skills” is the textbook signature phrase for Group Investigation. Recognising these two signature phrases instantly confirms both statements without needing to recall the full theory.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Jigsaw: each member specialises in one part of the content, then teaches it to the group.</li><li>Group Investigation: group-led inquiry – planning, investigating, and synthesising – is the core focus.</li><li>Both statements are accurate; answer is “both correct”.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>JIGSAW = puzzle piece per person; INVESTIGATION = detectives asking questions.</b> Picture a jigsaw puzzle (one unique piece per student) versus a group of detectives investigating a case (inquiry-driven) to keep the two strategies apart instantly.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Confusing Jigsaw with Group Investigation because both are “group work” – the distinguishing test is always <i>how</i> the group divides labour: by content-specialisation (Jigsaw) or by open-ended inquiry process (Group Investigation).</div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_d',no:9,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> Implicit memory refers to intentional or conscious recollection of information.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> Explicit memory refers to memories people are not consciously aware of, but that can affect their subsequent performance and behaviour.<br> In light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate</i> answer from the options given below',opts:['A) Both statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect','D) statement I incorrect but statement II is correct'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Concept required:</b> <b>Explicit (declarative) memory</b> is <i>intentional, conscious</i> recollection (facts, events – e.g. recalling a phone number on purpose). <b>Implicit (non-declarative) memory</b> consists of memories <i>people are not consciously aware of</i>, yet which still influence later performance/behaviour (e.g. priming, procedural skills like riding a bicycle). The two statements in this question have exactly <i>swapped</i> these standard definitions.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> Statement I attributes “intentional/conscious recollection” to <i>implicit</i> memory; Statement II attributes “not consciously aware of, but affects performance” to <i>explicit</i> memory.</li><li><b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Check whether each label (implicit/explicit) has been matched to its <i>correct</i> standard definition.</li><li><b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Standard cognitive-psychology definitions: explicit memory = conscious/intentional; implicit memory = unconscious, but performance-influencing.</li><li><b>Step 4 (Evaluate Statement I):</b> “Intentional or conscious recollection” is, by definition, <b>explicit</b> memory, not implicit – so Statement I mislabels the concept and is <b>False</b>.</li><li><b>Step 5 (Evaluate Statement II):</b> “Not consciously aware of, but affects performance” is, by definition, <b>implicit</b> memory, not explicit – so Statement II also mislabels the concept and is <b>False</b>.</li><li><b>Step 6 (Combine):</b> Both statements individually describe a real memory type correctly <i>in content</i>, but attach the <i>wrong label</i> to it – making both statements, as worded, false.</li><li><b>Step 7 (Correct option):</b> Both incorrect matches <b>Option 2</b>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Conceptual explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">This item tests precise vocabulary control, not just conceptual familiarity. A candidate who recognises “conscious, intentional” and “unconscious, but performance-affecting” as valid descriptions of memory <i>types</i> might rush to mark “both correct” – missing that the specific implicit/explicit <i>labels</i> have been deliberately interchanged.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Explanation of every option &amp; why the others are incorrect</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — both correct – wrongly accepts the swapped labels as if the definitions matched the correct memory type; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> — both incorrect – correctly identifies that each statement has attached its (valid) definition to the <i>wrong</i> memory-type label; correct.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — I correct, II incorrect – wrongly accepts Statement I even though “intentional/conscious” describes explicit, not implicit, memory; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — I incorrect, II correct – wrongly accepts Statement II even though “unconscious but performance-affecting” describes implicit, not explicit, memory; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Justification of the correct answer</div><div class="p1x-b">Swapping the standard content of Statement I with Statement II produces two <i>true</i> statements (“explicit memory is intentional/conscious”; “implicit memory is memory people are not consciously aware of but which affects performance”); since the question as printed keeps the labels reversed, both printed statements are false, confirming Option 2.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Alternative solution method</div><div class="p1x-b">Anchor on the everyday example test: try substituting a concrete example. “Riding a bicycle without consciously thinking about it” is a textbook implicit-memory example, yet Statement I claims implicit memory is “intentional/conscious” – the example immediately contradicts the statement, flagging it as false; the same test applied to Statement II (recalling a friend’s birthday deliberately = explicit, not what Statement II claims) confirms the second statement is also false.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Explicit memory = conscious, intentional recollection (facts, events).</li><li>Implicit memory = unconscious memory that still affects performance/behaviour (skills, priming).</li><li>This question has swapped the two labels in both statements – both are therefore false.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>ex-PLICIT = ex-PLICITLY aware.</b> “Explicit” shares its root with “explicitly stated” – i.e. consciously, deliberately known. If a definition talks about conscious/intentional recall, the correct label is always <i>explicit</i>, never implicit.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Reading only the <i>content</i> of a definition-matching statement and agreeing it “sounds right”, without checking that the specific <i>label</i> (implicit/explicit) attached to it is the correct one – always verify the label-definition pairing word-by-word.</div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_d',no:10,q:'Schuman’s model is an example of which type of teaching model?',opts:['A) Personal Basis Model','B) Behaviour Altering Model','C) Information Development Model','D) Social Interaction Model'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Concept required:</b> Teaching models are traditionally grouped into four families: <b>Information Processing/Development Models</b> (focused on how learners acquire, organise and process information – e.g. Concept Attainment, Inductive Thinking, and Schuman’s model), <b>Personal/Personal-Source Models</b> (focused on the individual’s self-development, e.g. non-directive teaching), <b>Social Interaction Models</b> (focused on relationships and group processes, e.g. Jurisprudential model), and <b>Behaviour Modification Models</b> (focused on observable behaviour change, e.g. mastery learning, operant conditioning-based models).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> Schuman’s teaching model is one of several named models under the broader four-family taxonomy of teaching models.</li><li><b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Identify which of the four families Schuman’s model belongs to.</li><li><b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Match Schuman’s model’s central concern (organising and developing information/inquiry skills in learners) to the family definition it best fits.</li><li><b>Step 4–6 (Match against each family):</b> Schuman’s model centres on structured information acquisition and cognitive processing, which is the hallmark of the <b>Information Development (Information Processing) family</b>, not personal-growth, not group/social-relations, and not overt behaviour-modification.</li><li><b>Step 7 (Correct option):</b> Information Development Model matches <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Conceptual explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The four-family classification (Information Processing, Personal, Social Interaction, Behaviour Modification) is the standard organising scheme (after Joyce &amp; Weil) used in Indian teacher-education and NET syllabi. Any model whose central mechanism is the learner’s handling, organising, and developing of information/knowledge structures is filed under the Information Processing/Development family – exactly Schuman’s model’s concern.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Explanation of every option &amp; why the others are incorrect</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — Personal Basis Model – this family targets an individual’s personal/emotional growth and self-actualisation (e.g. non-directive teaching), not information development; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — Behaviour Altering Model – this family targets observable, measurable behaviour change through reinforcement, unrelated to Schuman’s information-oriented focus; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> — Information Development Model – correctly places Schuman’s model within the information-processing family; correct.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — Social Interaction Model – this family targets group/interpersonal processes (e.g. role-play, group investigation), not individual information development; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Justification of the correct answer</div><div class="p1x-b">Since Schuman’s model’s defining goal is the systematic development and organisation of information/knowledge in the learner’s mind, it fits the Information Development family definition precisely, and no other family’s defining goal matches.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Alternative solution method</div><div class="p1x-b">Process-of-elimination by family keyword: eliminate “Social Interaction” (no group/relationship focus mentioned), eliminate “Behaviour Altering” (no reinforcement/behaviour-change focus mentioned), eliminate “Personal Basis” (no self-actualisation focus mentioned) – leaving Information Development as the only remaining, consistent family.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Four families of teaching models: Information Processing/Development, Personal, Social Interaction, Behaviour Modification.</li><li>Schuman’s model belongs to the Information Development family.</li><li>Match a model to its family by asking: what is its <i>central</i> mechanism – information, self, social relations, or behaviour?</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Exam tip</div><div class="p1x-b">Build a simple four-column revision chart with 2–3 named models under each family (Information Processing: Concept Attainment, Inductive Thinking, Schuman; Personal: Non-directive teaching; Social: Jurisprudential, Role-play; Behaviour: Mastery Learning) – family-classification questions become trivial recall once this chart is memorised.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Guessing a model’s family purely from its name sounding “social” or “personal” without checking its actual documented mechanism – always anchor the classification to the model’s stated central goal, not to superficial wording.</div></div>'},
  // ── 08 Dec 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_c',no:7,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> The process of comparing a student’s performance against his/her previous performance is called as ipsative assessment.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> Authentic assessments are assessment procedures that test skills and abilities as they would be applied in real life situations.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate answer</i> from the options given below:',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','D) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Evaluation systems in higher education (Unit I of the syllabus) classify assessment by the “reference frame” used for comparison: <b>norm-referenced</b> (comparing a student to peers), <b>criterion-referenced</b> (comparing to a fixed standard), and <b>ipsative</b> (comparing a student only to his/her own earlier performance, tracking self-improvement). <b>Authentic assessment</b> is a separate design principle: it evaluates learners through tasks that mirror real-world application of the skill being tested (projects, portfolios, performance tasks), as opposed to decontextualised paper-pencil recall tests.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Recall the definition of ipsative assessment: it is self-referenced – the student’s present performance is measured against his/her own past performance, not against other students or an external standard.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Compare this definition with Statement I: it matches exactly, so Statement I is <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Recall the definition of authentic assessment: it uses real-life, applied tasks (e.g., solving an actual problem, conducting an experiment, building a product) so that what is tested resembles what will actually be used outside the classroom.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Compare this definition with Statement II: it matches exactly, so Statement II is <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> Since both textbook definitions are stated correctly and without any distortion, both statements are correct.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> Correct option: Option (1), “Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.”</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Both statements are standard, textbook-accurate definitions – ipsative assessment is self-comparative (present vs. own past performance) and authentic assessment is real-life/applied in nature. Neither statement contains a distortion, so both are correct, making Option (1) the answer.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – both definitions given are textbook-accurate.</li><li><b>Option (2)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – would be true only if both definitions were wrong, but neither is; this option tests whether the candidate has confused ipsative with norm-referenced assessment.</li><li><b>Option (3)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly assumes Statement II is flawed; authentic assessment genuinely does mean testing skills as they would be applied in real life.</li><li><b>Option (4)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly assumes Statement I is flawed; ipsative assessment genuinely does mean self-comparison against one’s own past performance, not comparison to peers (which would instead be norm-referenced assessment).</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Ipsative assessment = compares a learner to their own earlier performance (self-referenced).</li><li>Norm-referenced assessment = compares a learner to peers; Criterion-referenced = compares to a fixed standard.</li><li>Authentic assessment = evaluates through real-life, applied tasks rather than decontextualised recall questions.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_c',no:8,q:'Which of the following are the types/sub-types of long term memory?<br> (A) Declarative memory<br> (B) Procedural memory<br> (C) Episodic memory<br> (D) Semantic memory<br> Choose the <i>correct</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A), (C) and (D) Only','B) (B) and (C) Only','C) (A) and (D) Only','D) (A), (B), (C) and (D)'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Long-Term Memory (LTM) is classically divided into two top-level types: <b>Declarative (Explicit) memory</b> – memory for facts and events that can be consciously recalled and “declared” – and <b>Procedural (Implicit/Non-declarative) memory</b> – memory for skills and how to perform actions, recalled unconsciously through performance. Declarative memory is further divided into two sub-types: <b>Episodic memory</b> (memory of personally experienced events, tied to time and place) and <b>Semantic memory</b> (memory of general facts, concepts and word meanings, independent of when/where they were learned).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> List the standard taxonomy of Long-Term Memory: LTM $\\rightarrow$ Declarative (Episodic + Semantic) and Procedural.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Check (A) Declarative memory: this is one of the two top-level types of LTM – <b>valid</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Check (B) Procedural memory: this is the other top-level type of LTM (implicit/skill memory) – <b>valid</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Check (C) Episodic memory: this is a sub-type nested within Declarative memory – <b>valid</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> Check (D) Semantic memory: this is the other sub-type nested within Declarative memory – <b>valid</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> Since the question asks for “types/sub-types” together (not just top-level types), all four – (A), (B), (C) and (D) – correctly belong to the LTM family.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> Correct option: Option (4).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The question deliberately uses the phrase “types/sub-types” so that both the top-level types (Declarative, Procedural) and the second-level sub-types nested inside Declarative memory (Episodic, Semantic) all qualify as correct answers. Hence all four items, (A) through (D), are valid, making Option (4) correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly excludes Procedural memory (B), which is very much a recognised type of long-term memory (the implicit/skill-based branch).</li><li><b>Option (2)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – keeps only two items and wrongly excludes Declarative (A) and Semantic (D) memory, which are equally valid.</li><li><b>Option (3)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly excludes both Procedural (B) and Episodic (C) memory.</li><li><b>Option (4)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – correctly includes all four recognised types/sub-types of long-term memory.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>LTM = Declarative (Explicit) + Procedural (Implicit).</li><li>Declarative memory further splits into Episodic (personal events) and Semantic (general facts/concepts).</li><li>A “types/sub-types” question intentionally accepts both levels of the hierarchy as correct.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_c',no:10,q:'Which of the following are the learner related factors that influence learning?<br> (A) Learner’s level of aspiration and achievement motivation<br> (B) Learner’s readiness and will power<br> (C) Basic potential of the learner<br> (D) Learner’s physical and mental health<br> (E) Personality traits and behaviour of teacher<br> Choose the <i>correct</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A), (B) and (E) Only','B) (A), (B), (C) and (D) Only','C) (C) and (E) Only','D) (C), (D) and (E) Only'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Factors affecting teaching-learning (Unit I of the syllabus) are conventionally grouped under: factors related to the <b>Learner</b> (motivation, readiness, aptitude/potential, health), factors related to the <b>Teacher</b> (personality, competence, teaching style), factors related to <b>Support material/Institution</b> (resources, environment), and so on. To answer this question correctly, each listed item must be sorted into “learner-related” or “not learner-related.”</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Check (A) Level of aspiration and achievement motivation: this is an internal, motivational attribute <i>of the learner</i> – <b>learner-related, valid</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Check (B) Readiness and will power: this is the learner’s own state of preparedness – <b>learner-related, valid</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Check (C) Basic potential of the learner: this is the learner’s aptitude/intelligence – <b>learner-related, valid</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Check (D) Physical and mental health: this is a characteristic of the learner’s own well-being that affects learning capacity – <b>learner-related, valid</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> Check (E) Personality traits and behaviour of <i>teacher</i>: this explicitly describes the <b>teacher</b>, not the learner – <b>not learner-related, invalid</b> for this question.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> Collect the valid items: (A), (B), (C) and (D) Only.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> Correct option: Option (2).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Four of the five listed items – aspiration/motivation, readiness/will power, basic potential, and physical/mental health – are all attributes belonging to the learner and directly influence how that learner learns. Item (E) is a distractor because it describes the <i>teacher’s</i> personality and behaviour, which is a teacher-related factor, not a learner-related one, so it must be excluded, leaving Option (2) as correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly includes (E), a teacher-related factor, while dropping two genuine learner factors, (C) and (D).</li><li><b>Option (2)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – correctly captures all four genuine learner-related factors and excludes the teacher-related item (E).</li><li><b>Option (3)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly includes (E) and drops three genuine learner-related items, (A), (B) and (D).</li><li><b>Option (4)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly includes (E) alongside only two genuine learner factors, dropping (A) and (B).</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Learner factors: aspiration/motivation, readiness/will power, basic potential (aptitude), physical and mental health.</li><li>Teacher’s personality and behaviour is a <i>teacher</i>-related factor, not a learner-related one – a frequently repeated NTA trap.</li><li>Always sort each listed item by “whose attribute is this?” before selecting the combination option.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 07 Dec 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_b',no:6,q:'The learning, in which a new behaviour is acquired but is not demonstrated until reinforcement is provided, is known as',opts:['A) Observational learning','B) Social learning','C) Latent learning','D) Moral learning'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Latent Learning (E.C. Tolman).</b> Tolman’s classic maze experiments with rats showed that learning can occur <i>without</i> any observable change in behaviour and without immediate reinforcement. The learning remains ‘hidden’ (latent) until an incentive/reinforcement is introduced, at which point performance suddenly improves – demonstrating that learning and performance are separable processes (the ‘cognitive map’ hypothesis).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">By definition, learning that is acquired but stays unexpressed until reinforcement appears is <b>Latent Learning</b>, first demonstrated by Tolman &amp; Honzik (1930) in rats that clearly had learned the maze layout during the unrewarded phase, but only showed it once food reward was introduced.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b><b>Option (A)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Learning by watching a model perform a behaviour and later imitating it (Bandura) – performance typically follows soon after observation, not delayed until reinforcement.</li><li><b><b>Option (B)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Learning that occurs through interaction within a social context/observation of others; related to but broader than pure observational learning; not specifically about delayed demonstration.</li><li><b><b>Option (C)</b> – <span class="p1-green">✓ CORRECT</span></b> <b>Correct.</b> Learning is acquired covertly and remains undemonstrated until reinforcement is given – the precise definition of latent learning.</li><li><b><b>Option (D)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Learning of ethical/moral values and conduct through socialisation, modelling of moral exemplars, or cognitive-developmental stages (Kohlberg) – unrelated to the reinforcement-delay criterion in the question.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Latent learning $\\Rightarrow$ learning occurs but stays hidden until reinforced (Tolman’s rat-maze studies).</li><li>Key distinction: <i>learning</i> (an internal change) vs <i>performance</i> (its observable expression) – latent learning is the classic proof they are not identical.</li><li>Do not confuse with observational/social learning (Bandura), which concerns learning <i>by watching others</i>, not delayed demonstration.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_b',no:8,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> Statement (I): As per Dewey’s vision of learning, learners should demonstrate their knowledge through creativity and collaboration.<br> Statement (II): According to Piaget, learning is independent of the developmental stages of learner.<br> In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are correct.','B) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are incorrect.','C) Statement (I) is correct but Statement (II) is incorrect.','D) Statement (I) is incorrect but Statement (II) is correct.'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Dewey’s experientialism</b> treats learning as an active, social, creative and collaborative process (‘learning by doing’). <b>Piaget’s cognitive-developmental theory</b>, by contrast, holds that learning is closely tied to and <i>constrained by</i> the learner’s current stage of cognitive development (sensorimotor, pre-operational, concrete-operational, formal-operational) – learning is therefore <i>dependent</i> on, not independent of, developmental stage.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Statement (I) correctly reflects Dewey’s progressive-education view (creativity and collaboration as demonstrations of knowledge). Statement (II) is false because Piaget explicitly makes learning <i>stage-dependent</i>; hence Option (C) ‘I correct, II incorrect’ is the answer.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b><b>Option (A)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect because Statement (II) misrepresents Piaget, whose entire theory is built on stage-dependent cognitive development.</li><li><b><b>Option (B)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect because Statement (I) is a fair and standard summary of Dewey’s pedagogy.</li><li><b><b>Option (C)</b> – <span class="p1-green">✓ CORRECT</span></b> <b>Correct.</b> Statement (I) accurately reflects Dewey; Statement (II) inverts Piaget’s actual position.</li><li><b><b>Option (D)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – it is Statement (I), not (II), that is accurate.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Dewey: learning = active, experiential, social, and demonstrated through creativity/collaboration.</li><li>Piaget: learning is <i>dependent</i> on the learner’s developmental stage – a child cannot be taught concepts beyond their current cognitive stage.</li><li>Watch for ‘independent of’ vs ‘dependent on’ reversals – a favourite NTA trap in assertion-based theory questions.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_b',no:9,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> Statement (I): The non-directive model of teaching nurtures students rather than controlling the sequence of learning.<br> Statement (II): In the non-directive model of teaching the teacher’s role is that of a facilitator who has counselling relationship with students and who guides their growth and development.<br> In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are correct.','B) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are incorrect.','C) Statement (I) is correct but Statement (II) is incorrect.','D) Statement (I) is incorrect but Statement (II) is correct.'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Non-directive Model of Teaching (Carl Rogers).</b> Rooted in humanistic/client-centred counselling theory, this model shifts the teacher’s role from a controlling instructor to a facilitator/counsellor who creates a supportive, non-judgemental climate, allowing students to direct their own learning and personal growth.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Both statements accurately describe the non-directive model: it nurtures rather than controls (Statement I), and the teacher acts as a facilitator/counsellor guiding growth (Statement II). Hence Option (A) ‘Both correct’ is right.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b><b>Option (A)</b> – <span class="p1-green">✓ CORRECT</span></b> <b>Correct.</b> Both statements are textbook-accurate descriptions of Rogers’ non-directive teaching model.</li><li><b><b>Option (B)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – both statements are, in fact, accurate.</li><li><b><b>Option (C)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – Statement (II) is also correct, not incorrect.</li><li><b><b>Option (D)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – Statement (I) is also correct, not incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Non-directive model (Rogers): humanistic, learner-centred, teacher = facilitator/counsellor.</li><li>Contrast with directive models where the teacher controls sequencing, pacing and content delivery.</li><li>Key descriptors to associate with ‘non-directive’: nurture, facilitate, counsel, guide growth.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_b',no:10,q:'In Charlotte Danielson’s framework for teaching, the domain of ‘Planning and Preparation’ has the following components:<br> A. Organising physical space in classroom B. Setting instructional outcomes C. Designing student assessments D. Demonstrating knowledge of content and pedagogy E. Demonstrating knowledge of resources<br> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) C and E only','B) A, B and D only','C) B, C, D and E only','D) A, B, C, D and E'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Danielson Framework for Teaching (FFT)</b> has four domains: (1) Planning and Preparation, (2) Classroom Environment, (3) Instruction, (4) Professional Responsibilities. Domain 1 (Planning &amp; Preparation) components include: demonstrating knowledge of content &amp; pedagogy, demonstrating knowledge of students, setting instructional outcomes, demonstrating knowledge of resources, designing coherent instruction, and designing student assessments. ‘Organising physical space’ belongs instead to Domain 2 (Classroom Environment).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">B (setting instructional outcomes), C (designing assessments), D (knowledge of content/pedagogy) and E (knowledge of resources) all belong to Domain 1; A (organising physical space) belongs to Domain 2 (Classroom Environment). So the correct set is Option (C) ‘B, C, D and E only’.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b><b>Option (A)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – omits B and D, both genuine Domain-1 components.</li><li><b><b>Option (B)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – wrongly includes A (Classroom Environment domain) and omits C and E.</li><li><b><b>Option (C)</b> – <span class="p1-green">✓ CORRECT</span></b> <b>Correct.</b> B, C, D, E are all authentic Planning-and-Preparation components; A belongs to a different domain.</li><li><b><b>Option (D)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – wrongly includes A, which belongs to the Classroom Environment domain, not Planning and Preparation.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Danielson’s FFT: 4 domains – Planning &amp; Preparation, Classroom Environment, Instruction, Professional Responsibilities.</li><li>Physical space organisation $\\Rightarrow$ Classroom Environment domain, <i>not</i> Planning &amp; Preparation.</li><li>Planning &amp; Preparation = content/pedagogy knowledge, student knowledge, outcomes, resources, coherent instruction, assessment design.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 06 Dec 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_a',no:6,q:'The organised bodies of information stored in memory that bias the way new information is interpreted, stored, and recalled is known as:',opts:['A) Constructive process','B) Schemas','C) Chunks','D) Association'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">A <b>schema</b> (plural <i>schemas</i>/<i>schemata</i>) is an organised, pre-existing structure of knowledge held in long-term memory. Schema theory (rooted in the work of Bartlett and later Piaget) holds that people do not store new information in a vacuum: incoming information is filtered through, and actively distorted by, whatever relevant schema is already active in memory. This is why two people can perceive, encode and later recall the very same event quite differently – their prior schemas bias interpretation, storage and recall.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> The question describes “organised bodies of information stored in memory” that “bias” interpretation, storage and recall of new information.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Identify the single technical term from educational/cognitive psychology that matches this exact description.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Compare the description against the standard definitions of the four options – constructive process, schema, chunk, and association.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 (Eliminate Constructive Process):</b> A “constructive process” refers broadly to the general act of building/constructing meaning; it is a process, not an “organised body of information” – it does not fit the noun-based description in the question.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 (Eliminate Chunk and Association):</b> A “chunk” (Miller, 1956) is a small grouped unit of information used to overcome short-term memory capacity limits (7 $\\pm$ 2 items) – it concerns capacity, not bias. An “association” is simply a learned link between two stimuli/ideas – it is a single connection, not an organised structure.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 (Match to Schema):</b> Only “Schema” precisely matches: it is (a) an organised body of knowledge, (b) stored in memory, and (c) known specifically for biasing interpretation, encoding and recall of new information.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 (Correct option):</b> The correct option is (2) Schemas.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Schemas act as mental frameworks or templates. When new information arrives, the mind tries to fit it into an existing schema; information that matches the schema is encoded and recalled more easily, while information that conflicts with it may be distorted, ignored, or even falsely “remembered” to fit the schema. This exact biasing property – of interpretation, storage, and recall – is the defining feature of schemas, making option (2) the only fully correct match.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1) – Constructive process</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Describes the general act of building knowledge/meaning, not a stored “organised body of information”; too broad and not the specific technical term the question wants.</li><li><b>Option (2) – Schemas</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span>: Exactly matches the definition – an organised knowledge structure in memory that biases interpretation, storage and recall.</li><li><b>Option (3) – Chunks</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Chunking concerns grouping items to extend short-term memory capacity, not biasing interpretation of new information.</li><li><b>Option (4) – Association</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: An association is a single learned link between two items, not an “organised body” of information.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Schema = organised knowledge structure in long-term memory.</li><li>Schemas bias how new information is interpreted, stored and recalled.</li><li>Chunking concerns short-term memory capacity (7 $\\pm$ 2), not bias.</li><li>Association = a single learned link, not an organised structure.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_a',no:7,q:'Which of the following are the components of pedagogical analysis of teaching and learning? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">(A)</span> Objective formulation</li><li><span class="p1-lab">(B)</span> Selection of teaching methods and materials</li><li><span class="p1-lab">(C)</span> Content analysis</li><li><span class="p1-lab">(D)</span> Selection of evaluation devices</li></ol> Choose the <i>correct</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (B), (C) and (D) Only','B) (A) and (D) Only','C) (A), (B), (C) and (D)','D) (B) and (C) Only'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Pedagogical analysis</b> is a systematic instructional-design procedure used (especially in the NCERT/CIET model) to plan a teaching unit thoroughly before it is taught. It typically proceeds through: (i) content/task analysis of the topic, (ii) formulation of instructional objectives (often in behavioural terms), (iii) selection of appropriate teaching methods, strategies and materials to achieve those objectives, and (iv) selection of evaluation devices/tools to check whether the objectives were achieved. All four stages together constitute a complete pedagogical analysis.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Recall the standard four-stage model of pedagogical analysis: content analysis $\\rightarrow$ objective formulation $\\rightarrow$ selection of teaching methods/materials $\\rightarrow$ selection of evaluation devices.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Check (A) Objective formulation – a core, textbook-defined stage of pedagogical analysis – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Check (B) Selection of teaching methods and materials – a core stage (choosing <i>how</i> to teach) – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Check (C) Content analysis – the very first stage (breaking the topic into teachable concepts/facts/skills) – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> Check (D) Selection of evaluation devices – the final stage (deciding how learning will be assessed) – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> Since all four (A), (B), (C) and (D) are genuine, standard components, the complete set must be selected.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> This matches option (3).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Pedagogical analysis is deliberately comprehensive – it spans planning <i>what</i> to teach (content analysis), <i>why</i> (objectives), <i>how</i> (methods and materials), and <i>how learning will be checked</i> (evaluation devices). None of the four listed components can be dropped without leaving the analysis incomplete, so the fully inclusive option (3) is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1) – (B), (C) and (D) Only</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Wrongly excludes (A) Objective formulation, which is an essential, textbook-recognised stage.</li><li><b>Option (2) – (A) and (D) Only</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Wrongly excludes both (B) and (C), leaving out method selection and content analysis, both core stages.</li><li><b>Option (3) – (A), (B), (C) and (D)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span>: Correctly includes all four genuine components of pedagogical analysis.</li><li><b>Option (4) – (B) and (C) Only</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Wrongly excludes both (A) and (D), omitting objective formulation and evaluation-device selection.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Pedagogical analysis has four components: content analysis, objective formulation, method/material selection, evaluation-device selection.</li><li>All four (A)–(D) in this question are genuine components – none should be excluded.</li><li>Think of it as a WHAT $\\rightarrow$ WHY $\\rightarrow$ HOW $\\rightarrow$ HOW-CHECKED planning cycle.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_a',no:8,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> In teacher centred learning the teacher gives up the traditional decision making role and chooses instead the role of a facilitator who focuses on students’ feelings.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> In learner centred method the teacher attempts to see the world as the students see it, creating an atmosphere of empathetic communication.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','D) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">In <b>teacher-centred</b> instruction, the teacher retains firm control of content, pace and decision-making; the teacher is the primary authority and source of knowledge. In <b>learner-centred</b> instruction, the teacher deliberately shares or gives up some of that traditional decision-making role, acting instead as a <i>facilitator</i> who tries to understand the world from the learners’ point of view and communicates empathetically with them.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Re-read Statement I carefully: it describes a teacher who “gives up the traditional decision-making role” and becomes a “facilitator who focuses on students’ feelings” – but it labels this as <b>teacher-centred</b> learning.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Compare this behaviour with the accepted definitions: giving up control and becoming a facilitator focused on learners’ feelings is the defining behaviour of <b>learner-centred</b>, not teacher-centred, teaching.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Since Statement I mislabels a learner-centred behaviour as “teacher centred”, Statement I is <b>False</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Re-read Statement II: it says that in learner-centred method, the teacher tries to see the world as students see it and creates an atmosphere of empathetic communication – this is exactly the standard, correct description of learner-centred teaching (associated with humanistic educators such as Carl Rogers).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> Statement II is therefore <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> Combination: Statement I incorrect, Statement II correct $\\Rightarrow$ option (4).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The question hinges on a single word: the behaviour in Statement I (giving up control, becoming a facilitator) is real and correctly described – <i>but it is wrongly attributed to “teacher centred” learning</i>, when it is in fact the hallmark of learner-centred learning. Statement II uses the correct label (“learner centred”) for essentially the same idea, and its description is accurate, so only Statement II survives as correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1) – Both correct</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Statement I mislabels a learner-centred trait as teacher-centred, so it cannot be correct.</li><li><b>Option (2) – Both incorrect</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Statement II is an accurate description of learner-centred teaching, so it is not incorrect.</li><li><b>Option (3) – I correct, II incorrect</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Reverses the true situation; I is the incorrect one and II is the correct one.</li><li><b>Option (4) – I incorrect, II correct</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span>: Matches the analysis exactly – I mislabels the approach, II correctly describes it.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Do not judge Statement I only by whether its <i>description</i> of the behaviour sounds reasonable – also check whether the <i>label</i> (teacher-centred vs learner-centred) attached to that behaviour is the correct one. NTA frequently tests exactly this kind of “correct description, wrong label” trap.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Teacher-centred: teacher retains control and decision-making authority.</li><li>Learner-centred: teacher becomes a facilitator, gives up some control, empathises with students’ perspective.</li><li>Statement I wrongly labels a learner-centred behaviour as teacher-centred – False.</li><li>Statement II correctly describes learner-centred teaching – True.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_a',no:9,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> The ability to generate unusual, yet appropriate, responses to problems or questions is known as convergent thinking.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> The ability to produce responses that are based primarily on knowledge and logic is known as divergent thinking.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','D) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Divergent thinking</b> is the ability to generate multiple, unusual, yet appropriate/creative responses to a single open-ended problem (associated with creativity, J.P. Guilford’s Structure of Intellect model). <b>Convergent thinking</b> is the ability to apply logic and existing knowledge to narrow down many possibilities to a single, correct/best response. The two terms are opposites, and this question deliberately swaps their definitions.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Recall correct definitions: Divergent thinking = generating many unusual, appropriate responses (creativity); Convergent thinking = using knowledge/logic to arrive at one correct response.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Check Statement I: it defines “generate unusual, yet appropriate, responses” as <b>convergent</b> thinking – but this is actually the definition of <b>divergent</b> thinking. Statement I is <b>False</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Check Statement II: it defines “responses based primarily on knowledge and logic” as <b>divergent</b> thinking – but this is actually the definition of <b>convergent</b> thinking. Statement II is <b>False</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Both statements have swapped the two terms; both are incorrect.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> This matches option (2).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Both statements describe genuine psychological constructs correctly <i>in content</i> but attach the <i>wrong label</i> to each – Statement I’s description belongs to divergent thinking, not convergent; Statement II’s description belongs to convergent thinking, not divergent. Since both labels are swapped, both statements are false, making option (2) the only correct choice.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1) – Both correct</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Both statements have the labels reversed, so neither is correct as worded.</li><li><b>Option (2) – Both incorrect</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span>: Correctly identifies that both statements swap convergent and divergent thinking.</li><li><b>Option (3) – I correct, II incorrect</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Statement I is also mislabelled (it describes divergent thinking, not convergent), so I cannot be correct.</li><li><b>Option (4) – I incorrect, II correct</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Statement II is also mislabelled (it describes convergent thinking, not divergent), so II cannot be correct.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Do not assume that because a statement’s <i>description</i> sounds like a textbook definition, the <i>label</i> attached to it is automatically correct – NTA frequently swaps paired/opposite terms (convergent/divergent, formative/summative, reliability/validity) to test careful reading.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Divergent thinking = many unusual, creative, appropriate responses.</li><li>Convergent thinking = one correct response via logic and existing knowledge.</li><li>Statement I actually describes divergent thinking (mislabelled convergent) – False.</li><li>Statement II actually describes convergent thinking (mislabelled divergent) – False.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'dec23_a',no:10,q:'Given below are two statements: one is labelled as <b>Assertion (A)</b> and the other is labelled as <b>Reason (R)</b>.<br> <b>Assertion (A):</b> No test provides a perfect picture of a student’s abilities and skills.<br> <b>Reason (R):</b> A test is only one small sample of a student’s performance.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','B) Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is <b>not</b> the correct explanation of (A)','C) (A) is correct but (R) is not correct','D) (A) is not correct but (R) is correct'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">A core principle of educational measurement and evaluation is that any single test is, by design, a <b>sample</b> of a much larger domain of a student’s knowledge, skills and abilities – it can never exhaustively measure everything a student knows or can do. Because a test only samples behaviour, it inherently cannot give a perfect, complete picture of ability; this is exactly why multiple assessment tools (tests, projects, oral work, portfolios) are recommended in Continuous and Comprehensive Evaluation (CCE).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Evaluate Assertion (A): “No test provides a perfect picture of a student’s abilities and skills.” This is a well-established, true principle in educational measurement.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Evaluate Reason (R): “A test is only one small sample of a student’s performance.” This is also true – a test necessarily samples a limited set of items/tasks from the full domain of possible performance.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Check the logical link: does (R) explain <i>why</i> (A) is true? Since a test can only sample a small part of overall performance, it logically follows that it cannot capture the complete/perfect picture of ability – so (R) directly and correctly explains (A).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Since both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) explains (A), the correct combination is option (1).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The relationship between (A) and (R) here is a direct cause-and-effect (sampling limitation $\\rightarrow$ incomplete picture): because a test can only ever sample a portion of what a student knows and can do, it logically cannot give a perfect, exhaustive picture of the student’s true abilities and skills. This is precisely what makes (R) the correct explanation of (A), not just a separately true but unrelated fact.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1) – Both correct, R explains A</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span>: Both statements are true, and the sampling nature of testing (R) directly explains why no test is perfect (A).</li><li><b>Option (2) – Both correct, R does not explain A</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Wrongly denies the clear causal link between R and A.</li><li><b>Option (3) – A correct, R incorrect</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: R is in fact a true, well-established principle of testing, not incorrect.</li><li><b>Option (4) – A incorrect, R correct</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: A is in fact a true, well-established principle of testing, not incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>No single test can perfectly capture a student’s full abilities and skills.</li><li>A test is only a sample of overall performance – this is exactly why it is imperfect.</li><li>Sampling limitation (R) is the direct explanation for imperfection of testing (A).</li><li>This is why continuous and multiple assessment methods are recommended over a single test.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 19 Jun 2023 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_e',no:6,q:'Which of the following are the secondary laws of learning? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> Law of multiple response</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> Law of Associative shifting</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> Law of effect</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> Law of Analogy</li><li><span class="p1-lab">E.</span> Law of exercise</li></ol> Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) B, C and E only','B) A, B and D only','C) A, B and E only','D) C, D and E only'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">E.L. Thorndike’s Connectionism (trial-and-error learning) proposes <b>three primary laws</b> – Law of Readiness, Law of Exercise (use/disuse), and Law of Effect – which explain the basic stamping-in/stamping-out of a stimulus-response (S–R) bond. Alongside these, Thorndike also proposed <b>five secondary (subsidiary) laws</b> that refine and extend the primary laws: (i) Law of Multiple Response, (ii) Law of Set/Attitude, (iii) Law of Prepotency of Elements, (iv) Law of Response by Analogy, and (v) Law of Associative Shifting.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Given:</b> Five statements (A–E) describing laws of learning; need to identify which belong to the <i>secondary/subsidiary</i> category rather than the <i>primary</i> category.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Classify A – Law of multiple response:</b> This is one of the five secondary laws – a learner tries multiple responses (varied reactions) until the correct one that produces satisfaction is found. <b>Secondary law.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Classify B – Law of Associative shifting:</b> Also a secondary law – a response can be shifted from one stimulus to an entirely new, associated stimulus. <b>Secondary law.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Classify C – Law of effect:</b> This is one of Thorndike’s three <i>primary</i> laws (responses followed by satisfaction are strengthened; those followed by discomfort are weakened). <b>Primary law, not secondary.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Classify D – Law of Analogy:</b> This is the secondary "Law of Response by Analogy" – a learner responds to a new situation the way they would respond to a similar, previously-learnt situation. <b>Secondary law.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Classify E – Law of exercise:</b> This is one of the three <i>primary</i> laws (practice strengthens a bond; disuse weakens it). <b>Primary law, not secondary.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Conclusion:</b> The true secondary laws among the five are A, B and D – this is Option 2.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Options C and E name two of the three <i>primary</i> laws of learning (Effect and Exercise), so any answer combination that includes C or E as a "secondary" law is automatically wrong. Only A (Multiple Response), B (Associative Shifting) and D (Analogy) are genuinely secondary/subsidiary laws, matching Option 2 exactly.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – B, C and E only – correctly includes B but wrongly includes C and E, which are primary laws, not secondary ones.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – A, B and D only – exactly the three genuine secondary laws named in the statement set (Multiple Response, Associative Shifting, Analogy).</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – A, B and E only – correctly includes A and B but wrongly includes E (Law of Exercise), which is primary.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – C, D and E only – correctly includes D but wrongly includes both C and E, which are primary laws.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Primary 3 = "READY, EXERCISE, EFFECT"</b> (Readiness, Exercise, Effect – the original trio). Everything else Thorndike proposed afterwards to refine these three is <b>secondary</b>: Multiple response, Set/attitude, Prepotency of elements, Response by Analogy, Associative shifting – remember as <b>"MR. SPA"</b> (Multiple Response, Set, Prepotency, Analogy, Associative shifting).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Students often assume "Law of Effect" sounds subsidiary/minor and mark it secondary – it is in fact one of the three foundational <i>primary</i> laws and is the single most frequently tested Thorndike law in NET Paper I.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Thorndike’s 3 primary laws: Readiness, Exercise, Effect.</li><li>Thorndike’s 5 secondary laws: Multiple Response, Set/Attitude, Prepotency of Elements, Response by Analogy, Associative Shifting.</li><li>In this question, A (Multiple response), B (Associative shifting) and D (Analogy) are secondary $\\Rightarrow$ Option 2.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_e',no:7,q:'A collection of the student’s work in an area, showing growth, self-reflection, and achievement is called as.',opts:['A) Positive practice','B) Personal development','C) Anecdotal record','D) Portfolios'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">A <b>Portfolio</b> is a purposeful, organised collection of a student’s work assembled over time, which documents effort, progress and achievement in one or more areas. Unlike a single test score, a portfolio captures the <i>process</i> of learning (drafts, revisions, self-reflective notes) as well as the <i>product</i>, and is a hallmark tool of <b>authentic/alternative assessment</b> used in Choice Based Credit System evaluation and continuous comprehensive evaluation.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Given:</b> A definition – "a collection of student work in an area, showing growth, self-reflection, and achievement."</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Required:</b> Identify the assessment term that matches this definition exactly.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Match to terminology:</b> The three defining features in the question – (i) a <i>collection</i> of work, (ii) evidence of <i>growth over time</i>, and (iii) <i>self-reflection</i> – are the textbook-defining features of a <b>Portfolio</b> in educational assessment literature.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Eliminate distractors:</b> "Positive practice" is a behavioural-conditioning term, "Personal development" is a generic HR/counselling term, and an "Anecdotal record" is a single running note by an observer about a specific incident – none of these involve a compiled, growth-documenting <i>collection</i> of the student’s own work.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Conclusion:</b> The correct term is <b>Portfolio</b> – Option 4.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The definition given precisely mirrors the standard definition of portfolio assessment used in teacher-training and evaluation-systems literature: a purposeful, chronological collection of student work that evidences growth, reflection and achievement – hence Option 4 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Positive practice – a behaviour-modification technique (deliberately repeating a correct response), unrelated to compiling a work-collection.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Personal development – too generic; refers broadly to growth of an individual’s skills/personality, not specifically an assessment artefact.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Anecdotal record – a brief, factual note of a single observed incident/behaviour, not a compiled body of work over time.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Portfolios – exactly matches the given definition: collection + growth + self-reflection + achievement.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Exam tip</div><div class="p1x-b">Whenever a question mentions "collection of work over time" together with "self-reflection," the answer is almost always <b>Portfolio</b> – this is one of the most frequently tested evaluation-innovation terms in Unit 1.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Portfolio = purposeful collection of student work + growth + self-reflection + achievement.</li><li>It is a tool of authentic/alternative assessment, distinct from anecdotal records (single-incident notes).</li><li>Frequently paired in NET with other innovative-evaluation terms: rubrics, checklists, peer-assessment.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_e',no:8,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> Contiguity theory gives no place to effect in learning.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> Watson’s theory gave emphasis on the importance of frequency or exercise in learning.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','D) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Guthrie’s Contiguity Theory</b> holds that a stimulus and response become associated purely because they occur <i>together in time</i> (contiguously) – learning is one-trial and does not depend on reinforcement, reward, or "effect" at all; this is a direct departure from Thorndike’s Law of Effect. Separately, <b>J.B. Watson</b>, the founder of Behaviourism, held that the <i>strength</i> of a stimulus-response habit depends chiefly on how often (<b>frequency</b>) and how recently the pairing has occurred, foreshadowing what later came to be debated against Guthrie’s one-trial view.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Evaluate Statement I:</b> Contiguity theory (Guthrie) explicitly rejects the Law of Effect – for Guthrie, mere temporal pairing of stimulus and response is sufficient for association; reinforcement/satisfaction plays no causal role. <b>Statement I is True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Evaluate Statement II:</b> Watson’s behaviourist account of habit formation emphasised that a response becomes attached to a stimulus in proportion to how frequently (and recently) the two have occurred together – i.e., frequency/exercise of the pairing strengthens the S–R bond. <b>Statement II is True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Combine:</b> Since both statements independently hold up against the standard account of these two theories, both are correct.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Conclusion:</b> Option 1 – Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Statement I is a textbook description of Guthrie’s Contiguity theory (association by simultaneity, no role for effect/reinforcement). Statement II correctly attributes an emphasis on frequency/exercise of stimulus-response pairing to Watson’s early behaviourist position, which historically preceded and was later contrasted with Guthrie’s one-trial contiguity view. Both being accurate, Option 1 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Both statements accurately represent their respective theories.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Wrongly rejects both statements, which are in fact standard, accurate descriptions.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Wrongly rejects Statement II; Watson did emphasise frequency/exercise in habit strength.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Wrongly rejects Statement I; Contiguity theory genuinely excludes "effect" as a factor in learning.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Do not confuse Guthrie’s Contiguity theory with Thorndike’s Law of Effect – Guthrie is famous <i>precisely because</i> he removed reinforcement from the explanation of learning, keeping only stimulus-response contiguity.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Contiguity theory (Guthrie): S–R association by simultaneity alone; no role for reinforcement/effect; one-trial learning.</li><li>Watson (Behaviourism): habit strength depends on frequency and recency of S–R pairing.</li><li>Both statements in this item are textbook-accurate $\\Rightarrow$ Option 1.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_e',no:10,q:'What are the different steps followed by the self-regulated learning? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> Setting goals</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> Analyzing the task</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> Regulating learning</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> Devising plans</li><li><span class="p1-lab">E.</span> Enacting tactics and strategies</li></ol> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) C, A, B, D, E','B) D, C, A, E, B','C) B, A, D, E, C','D) D, C, E, A, B'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Self-Regulated Learning (SRL)</b> (Zimmerman, Winne &amp; Hadwin) is a cyclical process in which a learner actively manages their own cognition, motivation and behaviour to achieve a learning goal. A learner must first <i>understand what is being asked</i> (analyse the task), then <i>decide what to achieve</i> (set goals), then <i>decide how</i> (devise plans and enact strategies/tactics), and finally <i>monitor and adjust</i> (regulate learning) based on how the plan is working.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Given:</b> Five SRL activities (A–E) in scrambled order.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Required:</b> The logical, textbook sequence of these activities as followed by a self-regulated learner.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Apply the SRL cycle logic:</b> A learner cannot set a meaningful goal until the task itself is understood, so <b>Analyzing the task (B)</b> must come first.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Once the task is understood, the learner decides what outcome to aim for – <b>Setting goals (A)</b> comes second.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> With a goal in place, the learner works out how to reach it – <b>Devising plans (D)</b> comes third.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> The learner then puts the plan into action – <b>Enacting tactics and strategies (E)</b> comes fourth.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> Finally, while and after acting, the learner monitors progress and adjusts – <b>Regulating learning (C)</b> comes last (fifth).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">8.</span> <b>Conclusion:</b> The correct order is B, A, D, E, C – Option 3.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Self-regulated learning follows a forethought $\\to$ performance $\\to$ self-reflection arc: understand the task, set a goal, plan how to reach it, execute the plan through concrete tactics, and regulate/monitor throughout and after. This maps exactly onto B (analyse) $\\to$ A (goals) $\\to$ D (plan) $\\to$ E (enact) $\\to$ C (regulate), which is Option 3.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – C, A, B, D, E – wrongly places "Regulating learning" first, before the task has even been analysed or a goal set.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – D, C, A, E, B – wrongly places "Devising plans" before the task is analysed and before a goal is set.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – B, A, D, E, C – Analyse $\\to$ Goal $\\to$ Plan $\\to$ Enact $\\to$ Regulate is the correct, textbook SRL sequence.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – D, C, E, A, B – places planning and regulation before the task is even analysed or a goal set, reversing the logical order.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>"ATP-ER"</b> — <b>A</b>nalyse the task, set <b>T</b>argets/goals, make a <b>P</b>lan, <b>E</b>nact it, then <b>R</b>egulate. Understand $\\to$ Aim $\\to$ Plan $\\to$ Act $\\to$ Adjust.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Self-Regulated Learning is cyclical: task analysis $\\to$ goal setting $\\to$ planning $\\to$ strategy enactment $\\to$ regulation/monitoring.</li><li>Correct sequence for this item: B (analyse) $\\to$ A (goals) $\\to$ D (plan) $\\to$ E (enact) $\\to$ C (regulate).</li><li>Regulation is always the monitoring/adjusting step and logically comes last in the listed activities, not first.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 16 Jun 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_a',no:6,q:'In which method of collaborative learning the main purpose is to develop listening skills in the students?',opts:['A) circle of voices','B) problem solving','C) Fishbowl strategy','D) Jigsaw strategy'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Collaborative learning uses several structured discussion formats, each engineered to build a specific social or cognitive skill. <b>Circle of Voices</b> gives every learner an uninterrupted turn to speak, building equitable participation. <b>Fishbowl strategy</b> arranges learners in two concentric circles – an inner circle that actively discusses while an outer circle silently observes and listens – explicitly designed to build focused, active <i>listening</i> skills before the outer circle takes its turn. <b>Jigsaw</b> builds interdependence by giving each learner a unique piece of content to master and teach to peers. <b>Problem solving</b> groups build joint reasoning and negotiation skills.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Identify what each named strategy is structurally designed to develop: Circle of Voices $\\to$ equal speaking opportunity; Problem solving $\\to$ joint reasoning; Fishbowl $\\to$ an inner (speaking) and outer (listening/observing) circle; Jigsaw $\\to$ content interdependence and peer teaching.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Match the stated purpose in the question – "main purpose is to develop listening skills" – to the strategy whose defining structural feature is a dedicated listening/observing role.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Only the Fishbowl strategy has a built-in, explicit observer/listener sub-group (the outer circle) whose entire task during that round is to listen.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Conclude that Option 3 (Fishbowl strategy) is the correct answer.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The Fishbowl strategy is correct because its defining structural feature – an outer circle of learners who must listen attentively to the inner circle’s discussion (often to summarise, question, or later swap roles) – makes listening-skill development the explicit purpose of the technique, unlike the other three options whose primary purposes are participation equity, joint problem-solving, and content mastery/interdependence respectively.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (circle of voices):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Its purpose is equitable, uninterrupted speaking turns for every learner, not listening per se; tests whether the candidate confuses "structured turn-taking" with "listening skill development."</li><li><b>Option 2 (problem solving):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – This is a task-type (working jointly toward a solution), not a listening-focused structure; tests recognition that not every collaborative format targets a specific communication sub-skill.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Fishbowl strategy):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> – Its inner/outer circle design makes active listening the explicit role of part of the group.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Jigsaw strategy):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Its purpose is mutual interdependence and peer-teaching of unique content pieces, not listening skill per se; a commonly confused option because Jigsaw does involve some listening, but that is incidental, not its defining purpose.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Fishbowl = inner circle discusses, outer circle listens/observes $\\to$ builds listening skills.</li><li>Circle of Voices = equal, uninterrupted speaking turns $\\to$ builds participation equity.</li><li>Jigsaw = unique content pieces taught to peers $\\to$ builds interdependence.</li><li>Problem solving groups = joint reasoning toward a shared solution.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_a',no:7,q:'Which of the following Individual difference(s) may not affect learning?<br><br>A. Cultural background<br> B. Height<br> C. Blood Group<br> D. Age<br> E. Complexion<br><br>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, D and E only','B) B, C and E only','C) B only','D) A, C and D only'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Individual differences relevant to learning are those that influence a learner’s cognitive, social, or emotional engagement with instruction (per Unit-I of the syllabus: Academic, Social, Emotional and Cognitive characteristics). Purely physical/biological attributes that have no established causal link to cognitive processing – such as height, blood group, or complexion – do <i>not</i> affect learning. In contrast, cultural background (shapes prior knowledge, language, values, and social interaction patterns) and age (governs cognitive/developmental readiness, per Piagetian stages) are well-established, syllabus-recognised factors that <i>do</i> affect learning.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Classify each listed trait as either a genuine cognitive/social/emotional learner characteristic or a physically incidental attribute with no established influence on learning.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Cultural background (A) – affects learning (shapes language, prior knowledge, values); Height (B) – physical, no established effect; Blood Group (C) – purely biological, no established effect; Age (D) – affects learning (developmental/cognitive readiness); Complexion (E) – physical, no established effect.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Collect the traits that do <i>not</i> affect learning: B, C and E.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Match against the options – "B, C and E only" is Option 2.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Option 2 is correct because Height, Blood Group, and Complexion are physical/biological attributes with no recognised bearing on a learner’s cognitive processing, motivation, or academic performance, whereas Cultural background and Age are explicitly recognised individual-difference categories (social/cultural context and developmental stage respectively) that do influence how and what a person learns.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A, D and E only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Incorrectly places Cultural background (A) and Age (D), both genuine learning-affecting factors, alongside Complexion; tests whether the candidate wrongly treats a social/developmental factor as physically incidental.</li><li><b>Option 2 (B, C and E only):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> – Correctly isolates the three purely physical attributes with no established effect on learning.</li><li><b>Option 3 (B only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Under-selects; recognises Height correctly but misses that Blood Group and Complexion belong to the same "no effect" category.</li><li><b>Option 4 (A, C and D only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Incorrectly groups Cultural background and Age (both genuine factors) with Blood Group; tests the same confusion as Option 1 from the opposite direction.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Learner individual differences that matter: Academic, Social, Emotional, Cognitive (syllabus Unit-I) – Cultural background and Age fall here.</li><li>Purely physical/biological traits (Height, Blood Group, Complexion) have no established effect on learning.</li><li>Correct set of "does-not-affect-learning" traits: B, C, E $\\Rightarrow$ <b>Option 2</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_a',no:8,q:'Which of the following are the characteristics of Mind mapping?<br><br>A. Built around a central topic.<br> B. Use of long sentences and paragraphs to explain the content<br> C. The branches of a particular topic form a connected nodal structure.<br> D. Use of images and symbols to represent ideas.<br><br>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A only','B) B and D only','C) A, C and D only','D) B and C only'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Mind mapping (Tony Buzan) is a visual note-taking/organising technique with well-defined characteristics: it is always <b>built around one central topic or image</b>; ideas radiate outward as <b>branches that connect in a nodal (tree-like) structure</b>; and it relies heavily on <b>images, colours, symbols, and short keywords</b> rather than full sentences, because the visual/associative format is what makes mind maps fast to create and easy to recall. The use of long sentences and paragraphs is the opposite of mind mapping’s defining economy of expression.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the defining features of a mind map: central topic, radiating/branching nodal structure, and heavy use of images/symbols/keywords instead of prose.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Evaluate each statement: A (central topic) – true, defining feature; B (long sentences/paragraphs) – false, mind maps deliberately avoid this; C (branches form connected nodal structure) – true, defining feature; D (images and symbols) – true, defining feature.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Collect the true statements: A, C and D.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Match to the options – "A, C and D only" is Option 3.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Option 3 is correct because A, C, and D each describe a core, textbook-recognised structural feature of a mind map (central topic, connected branching structure, image/symbol-based representation), while B directly contradicts the technique’s purpose, since mind maps are specifically designed to condense ideas into brief, visually connected nodes rather than long, linear prose.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Correctly identifies the central-topic feature but under-selects, omitting two other equally valid defining characteristics (C and D).</li><li><b>Option 2 (B and D only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Incorrectly includes B, which describes conventional linear note-taking, the opposite of mind mapping’s visual/keyword-based approach.</li><li><b>Option 3 (A, C and D only):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> – Correctly selects all three genuine defining characteristics and excludes the one false statement.</li><li><b>Option 4 (B and C only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Incorrectly includes B (long sentences/paragraphs) while correctly including C; a partial-credit-style distractor testing whether the candidate can spot the one clearly false statement among otherwise plausible ones.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Mind maps: one central topic $\\to$ radiating branches $\\to$ connected nodal structure.</li><li>Represented via images, colours, symbols and keywords – <i>not</i> long sentences or paragraphs.</li><li>Correct characteristics here: A, C, D $\\Rightarrow$ <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_a',no:9,q:'Match List I with List II<br><br><div class="p1-blk"><div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th colspan="2"><b>LIST I (CHANNEL NO.)</b></th><th colspan="2"><b>LIST II (NAME)</b></th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>CH-04</td><td>I.</td><td>PRABANDHAN</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>CH-05</td><td>II.</td><td>KAUTILYA</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>CH-06</td><td>III.</td><td>SAARASWAT</td></tr><tr><td>D.</td><td>CH-07</td><td>IV.</td><td>VIDHIK</td></tr></table></div></div><br><br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','B) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','C) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','D) A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">This question is drawn from Unit-VIII (ICT) / Unit-X (Higher Education System) of the syllabus, testing awareness of <b>SWAYAM PRABHA</b>, the group of 32 DTH educational channels operated under India’s digital-initiatives-in-higher-education umbrella by the Consortium for Educational Communication (CEC) on behalf of UGC/MHRD. Several of these 32 channels are named after their subject focus in Hindi/Sanskrit-derived terms – e.g. <i>Saaraswat</i> (from Saraswati, goddess of learning – general/school education), <i>Prabandhan</i> (management studies), <i>Vidhik</i> (legal studies/law), and <i>Kautilya</i> (named for the ancient economist-statesman Chanakya/Kautilya – commerce and economics). Recognising the channel-number-to-subject-name mapping is a direct recall item.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall that SWAYAM PRABHA channels 04–07 are each dedicated to a specific higher-education subject cluster, identified by a distinct Hindi/Sanskrit-derived name.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Match each channel number to its designated subject name: CH-04 $\\to$ SAARASWAT (III); CH-05 $\\to$ PRABANDHAN (I); CH-06 $\\to$ VIDHIK (IV); CH-07 $\\to$ KAUTILYA (II).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Write the full mapping: A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Match against the options – this sequence is Option 3.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Option 3 is correct because it is the only sequence that correctly pairs each of the four channel numbers with its designated SWAYAM PRABHA subject name: CH-04 with Saaraswat, CH-05 with Prabandhan, CH-06 with Vidhik, and CH-07 with Kautilya.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Every single pairing is shifted from the correct mapping; tests whether the candidate has memorised the association at all versus guessing.</li><li><b>Option 2 (A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – A different full permutation with no pairing matching the correct list; a pure-recall distractor.</li><li><b>Option 3 (A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> – The fully correct channel-to-name mapping.</li><li><b>Option 4 (A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Gets C-IV (CH-06–Vidhik) coincidentally right but the remaining three pairings are incorrect; tests whether a candidate mistakes a partial match for the full correct answer.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>SWAYAM PRABHA is a bouquet of 32 DTH educational channels under India’s digital higher-education initiative (Unit-VIII/Unit-X).</li><li>CH-04 = Saaraswat, CH-05 = Prabandhan, CH-06 = Vidhik, CH-07 = Kautilya.</li><li>Correct match: A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II $\\Rightarrow$ <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_a',no:10,q:'Which of the following is a benefit of using rubrics for evaluation?',opts:['A) Greater ease of grading','B) Increased consistency in grading','C) Increased subjectivity','D) Greater test security'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">A rubric is a scoring guide that explicitly lists criteria and performance-level descriptors for an assessment task. Its primary pedagogical benefit (Unit-I: Evaluation Systems – Innovations in evaluation systems) is that it makes grading <b>criterion-referenced and transparent</b>, so that different evaluators (or the same evaluator at different times) apply the same standard to every learner’s work. This directly <b>increases consistency/reliability</b> of grading, reduces subjective, evaluator-to-evaluator variation, and improves fairness – it does not, by itself, make grading faster (it typically takes more time to apply criteria carefully) nor does it relate to test security.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the core purpose of a rubric: pre-defined, explicit criteria and performance descriptors applied uniformly across all responses/evaluators.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Evaluate each option against this purpose: "Greater ease of grading" – rubrics add structure but do not necessarily make grading <i>easier</i> or faster; "Increased consistency in grading" – directly matches the core purpose of standardising judgment; "Increased subjectivity" – the opposite of what rubrics achieve; "Greater test security" – unrelated to rubrics, which concern scoring, not test-paper security.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> The only option that describes rubrics’ actual, textbook-recognised benefit is consistency in grading.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Select Option 2.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Option 2 is correct because the defining pedagogical value of a rubric is that it standardises the criteria used to judge performance, so that grading becomes more consistent and reliable across markers and across time – this is the benefit most consistently cited in assessment literature and directly tested here.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Greater ease of grading):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – A commonly assumed but inaccurate benefit; applying detailed criteria carefully is often more time-consuming, not easier, than holistic/impressionistic grading.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Increased consistency in grading):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> – The genuine, textbook benefit of using explicit, shared scoring criteria.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Increased subjectivity):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Directly opposite to the actual effect of rubrics, which are designed specifically to <i>reduce</i> subjectivity; tests whether the candidate has inverted the concept.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Greater test security):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Confuses evaluation/scoring tools (rubrics) with test administration/security measures, which are an unrelated area of assessment practice.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>A rubric = explicit criteria + performance-level descriptors, applied uniformly.</li><li>Core benefit: increased consistency/reliability of grading across evaluators and over time.</li><li>Rubrics do <i>not</i> inherently make grading faster, nor do they concern test security.</li><li>Correct answer: <b>Option 2</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 15 Jun 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_b',no:6,q:'Which of the following are the examples of scaffolding? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> Providing hints and clues to solve a problem.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> Allowing the students to figure out the problem on their own.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> Providing the students with all the answers and solutions to a problem.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> Asking leading questions to guide the students thinking.</li></ol> Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) B and C only','B) A and D only','C) B and D only','D) A, B, C and D'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Scaffolding</b> (Vygotsky’s Zone of Proximal Development, popularised by Wood, Bruner &amp; Ross) is <i>temporary, adjustable support</i> that a teacher provides so a learner can accomplish a task slightly beyond their independent ability, with the support gradually withdrawn as competence grows. Genuine scaffolding techniques include: giving hints/clues, asking leading/guiding questions, modelling, prompting, and breaking a task into smaller steps – always leaving the learner to do the actual thinking and constructing of the answer.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the definition of scaffolding – it is <i>support that assists</i> thinking, not support that <i>replaces</i> thinking, and not the complete <i>absence</i> of support either.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Evaluate Statement A – giving hints and clues nudges the learner toward the answer while leaving the reasoning to them; this is textbook scaffolding. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Evaluate Statement B – leaving students to figure the problem out entirely on their own provides <i>no</i> support at all, so it is independent learning, not scaffolding. <b>False.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Evaluate Statement C – handing over all answers and solutions removes the learner’s need to think; this is direct instruction/spoon-feeding, the opposite of scaffolding. <b>False.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Evaluate Statement D – leading questions guide the learner’s thinking process step by step without giving the answer away; this is a classic scaffolding technique (Socratic questioning). <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Correct set $=\\{A, D\\}$, which is Option 2.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Scaffolding sits strictly between two extremes: (i) doing the work <i>for</i> the learner (Statement C) and (ii) leaving the learner with <i>no</i> support at all (Statement B). Only techniques that assist thinking without replacing it – hints/clues (A) and guiding/leading questions (D) – qualify as scaffolding, which is exactly Option 2.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (B and C only):</b> Both B (no support) and C (support that eliminates thinking) are opposite ends of the scaffolding spectrum, not examples of it; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2 (A and D only) – Correct:</b> Hints/clues and leading questions are precisely the kind of temporary, thinking-preserving support that defines scaffolding.</li><li><b>Option 3 (B and D only):</b> Correctly includes D but wrongly includes B, which describes the absence of scaffolding; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4 (A, B, C and D):</b> Over-inclusive – treats every listed teaching behaviour as scaffolding, ignoring that B and C fail the definition; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Scaffolding = temporary, adjustable support that preserves the learner’s own thinking (Vygotsky’s ZPD).</li><li>Hints, clues, prompts, modelling, and leading questions are genuine scaffolding techniques.</li><li>Giving the full answer (over-support) and giving no help at all (under-support) are both <i>not</i> scaffolding.</li><li>Correct answer: A and D only $\\rightarrow$ Option 2.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_b',no:7,q:'Which of the following are the humanistic approaches to learning? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> It emphasises on self-motivation for better learning.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> Its emphasis is on learning at the higher level.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> It fosters curiosity.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> It increases learner’s reactivity.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">E.</span> It believes that learning becomes effective when it is need-based.</li></ol> Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A and D only','B) B, C and E only','C) A, B, C, D and E','D) B and D only'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">The <b>Humanistic approach</b> (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) treats the learner as a whole person capable of self-directed growth. It centres teaching on the learner’s own motivation, curiosity, needs and self-actualisation, and treats the learner as an active, responsive participant rather than a passive recipient of instruction – in sharp contrast to behaviourist approaches, which stress external reinforcement and control.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> List the core commitments of humanistic learning theory: self-motivation, learner-centredness, curiosity, active/responsive engagement, and need-based/personally relevant learning.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Statement A (self-motivation) – central to Rogers’ notion of “significant learning” driven from within. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Statement B (learning at the higher level) – humanistic theory (echoing Maslow’s hierarchy) pushes learning toward higher-order growth and self-actualisation, not rote lower-order learning. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Statement C (fosters curiosity) – curiosity and intrinsic interest are treated as the engine of humanistic learning. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Statement D (increases learner’s reactivity/responsiveness) – humanistic classrooms are learner-centred and participative, increasing the learner’s active engagement and responsiveness rather than passive reception. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Statement E (need-based learning) – Rogerian/Maslowian theory explicitly holds that learning is most effective when it addresses the learner’s own felt needs. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> All five statements (A–E) are consistent with the humanistic approach, so the answer is Option 3.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Unlike many multi-statement NET items where a subset of statements is deliberately false, this question tests whether the candidate can recognise that <i>all five</i> listed features genuinely belong to humanistic learning theory – self-motivation, higher-order learning, curiosity, responsiveness, and need-based learning are all textbook hallmarks of the Rogers–Maslow humanistic tradition, so no statement needs to be excluded.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A and D only):</b> Under-inclusive – wrongly excludes B, C and E, which are equally valid humanistic features; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2 (B, C and E only):</b> Under-inclusive – wrongly excludes A and D; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3 (A, B, C, D and E) – Correct:</b> Recognises that every listed statement is a genuine feature of humanistic learning theory.</li><li><b>Option 4 (B and D only):</b> Severely under-inclusive, discarding three valid statements (A, C, E); incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Humanistic approach (Rogers, Maslow): learner-centred, self-motivated, need-based, curiosity-driven, higher-order growth.</li><li>Contrast with Behaviourism, which relies on external reinforcement rather than internal motivation.</li><li>In this item all five listed statements are valid humanistic features $\\rightarrow$ Option 3.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_b',no:8,q:'Which of the following is an example of innovative approach to evaluation systems?',opts:['A) Norm-referenced testing','B) Peer Evaluation','C) Standardized testing','D) Traditional grading'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Modern, innovative evaluation moves away from single, one-shot, teacher-only, norm/standardised testing toward <b>continuous, participative, formative</b> assessment: peer evaluation, self-assessment, portfolios, open-book/take-home assessment, and rubric-based grading. These innovations aim to make evaluation a learning experience in itself, not merely a ranking exercise.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Distinguish traditional/conventional evaluation tools from innovative ones.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Norm-referenced testing (Option 1) ranks students against each other using a fixed distribution – a long-standing, conventional technique, not an innovation.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Standardized testing (Option 3) and traditional grading (Option 4) are both conventional, teacher-centred, summative approaches used for decades.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Peer Evaluation (Option 2) is a participative, learner-involving technique that has been increasingly adopted as an innovative alternative assessment method, particularly emphasised for Choice Based Credit System (CBCS) and outcome-based education.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Correct option: Peer Evaluation – Option 2.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Peer evaluation shifts assessment responsibility partly onto learners themselves, developing metacognitive and critical-evaluation skills alongside subject learning – this participative, formative character is exactly what makes it an “innovative” approach as opposed to the purely summative, teacher-administered options.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Norm-referenced testing):</b> A conventional statistical technique comparing students against each other (e.g. grading on a curve); not innovative.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Peer Evaluation) – Correct:</b> A participative, formative technique widely cited as an innovation in higher-education evaluation systems.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Standardized testing):</b> A conventional, uniform, large-scale testing method; not innovative.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Traditional grading):</b> By definition the older, conventional method, the opposite of an innovative approach.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Innovative evaluation = participative + formative: peer evaluation, self-assessment, portfolios, open-book assessment.</li><li>Norm-referenced and standardized testing are conventional, comparison/ranking-based tools.</li><li>Correct answer: Peer Evaluation $\\rightarrow$ Option 2.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_b',no:9,q:'In swayamprabha, channel no. 08 (Physical sciences, Mathematics, Physics, Chemistry and related subjects) is named as -',opts:['A) Vageesh','B) Saaraswat','C) Aryabhatt','D) Prabandhan'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>SWAYAMPRABHA</b> is a Government of India (MoE/UGC) initiative offering 32 High Definition DTH channels dedicated to telecasting high-quality educational programmes 24x7, free of cost, using GSAT-15 satellite. Several individual channels are given subject-linked names honouring Indian scholarly tradition (e.g. channels devoted to Sanskrit/humanities, physical sciences, commerce/management, etc.), a fact explicitly listed under Unit-I (“Off-line vs. On-line methods: Swayam, Swayamprabha, MOOCs”) of the Paper I syllabus.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall that SWAYAMPRABHA channels are subject-thematically named, not numbered generically.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Channel 08 is dedicated to Physical Sciences, Mathematics, Physics, Chemistry and related subjects.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Among the options, “Aryabhatt” – named after the celebrated ancient Indian mathematician-astronomer Aryabhata – is the name officially assigned to this science/mathematics-oriented channel.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Correct option: Aryabhatt – Option 3.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">SWAYAMPRABHA channel names are deliberately drawn from Indian intellectual heritage matched to the channel’s subject focus; a mathematics-and-physical-science channel being named after Aryabhata (pioneer of algebra, place-value system and astronomy) is thematically consistent and is the officially notified name for Channel 08.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Vageesh):</b> Not the designated name for the physical sciences/mathematics channel; a distractor from a different channel’s theme.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Saaraswat):</b> Associated with language/literature-themed channels (from Saraswati, goddess of learning), not physical sciences; incorrect here.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Aryabhatt) – Correct:</b> The officially notified name for the Physical Sciences/Mathematics channel (Channel 08).</li><li><b>Option 4 (Prabandhan):</b> Associated with management/commerce-themed channels, not physical sciences; incorrect here.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>SWAYAMPRABHA: 32 DTH channels for free educational telecast, launched under Digital India / MoE.</li><li>Channels are named after Indian scholarly figures/concepts matched to their subject theme.</li><li>Channel 08 (Physical Sciences, Maths, Physics, Chemistry) $=$ “Aryabhatt” $\\rightarrow$ Option 3.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_b',no:10,q:'Which of the following ICT tools are associated with mind mapping? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> Mindmeister</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> X-mind</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> C-map</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> Screen casto-matic</li><li><span class="p1-lab">E.</span> Adobephotoshop</li></ol> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) B and D only','B) A, B and C only','C) A, C and E only','D) D and E only'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Mind/concept-mapping software</b> (Unit-I: “Teaching Support System: Traditional, Modern and ICT based”) lets teachers and learners visually organise ideas as branching diagrams. Purpose-built mind-mapping tools include MindMeister, XMind, and CmapTools. These must be distinguished from tools built for other purposes: Screencast-O-Matic is screen-recording software (for capturing lecture/demo videos), and Adobe Photoshop is raster image-editing software – neither is a mind-mapping tool.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Identify the function of each listed tool.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> MindMeister (A) – a dedicated cloud-based mind-mapping application. <b>Mind-mapping tool.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> X-Mind (B) – a dedicated desktop mind-mapping/brainstorming application. <b>Mind-mapping tool.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> C-Map / CmapTools (C) – concept-mapping software developed for building and sharing concept maps. <b>Mind/concept-mapping tool.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Screencast-O-Matic (D) – a screen-recording/video-capture tool, unrelated to mapping ideas. <b>Not a mind-mapping tool.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Adobe Photoshop (E) – an image/photo editing tool, unrelated to mapping ideas. <b>Not a mind-mapping tool.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> Correct set $=\\{A, B, C\\}$, which is Option 2.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The question is a straightforward tool-classification exercise: A, B and C are all software built specifically to draw and organise mind/concept maps, whereas D (screen recording) and E (image editing) serve entirely different ICT functions in the classroom and must be excluded.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (B and D only):</b> Wrongly includes D (a screen-recorder) and omits A and C, both genuine mind-mapping tools; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2 (A, B and C only) – Correct:</b> MindMeister, X-Mind and C-Map are all dedicated mind/concept-mapping applications.</li><li><b>Option 3 (A, C and E only):</b> Correctly includes A and C but wrongly includes E (Photoshop, an image editor); incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4 (D and E only):</b> Includes neither genuine mind-mapping tool and instead lists a screen-recorder and an image editor; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Mind-mapping ICT tools: MindMeister, X-Mind, CmapTools (concept maps).</li><li>Screencast-O-Matic = screen/video recording tool; Photoshop = image editing tool – neither is for mind-mapping.</li><li>Correct answer: A, B and C only $\\rightarrow$ Option 2.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 13 Jun 2023 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_c',no:6,q:'If Ram is not able to understand certain concept in a particular subject and is continuously performing poorly in that subject, which evaluation test will have to be conducted to know the cause of his difficulties?',opts:['A) Placement Evaluation','B) Diagnostic Evaluation','C) Summative Evaluation','D) Formative Evaluation'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Types of Evaluation.</b> <i>Placement evaluation</i> is done at the start of instruction to place a learner at the appropriate level/group. <i>Formative evaluation</i> is continuous, ongoing evaluation carried out <i>during</i> instruction to give feedback and improve teaching-learning in real time. <i>Summative evaluation</i> is done at the <i>end</i> of instruction, mainly for grading/certifying overall achievement. <i>Diagnostic evaluation</i> is a deeper, more specific evaluation used when a learner shows a <b>persistent, specific difficulty</b> – its purpose is to dig into <i>why</i> the learner is struggling and pinpoint the exact root cause (a gap in a prerequisite skill, a conceptual misunderstanding, etc.) so that targeted remediation can follow.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Identify the scenario:</b> Ram has a specific, subject-focused problem (not understanding a concept) that is <i>continuing</i> over time (continuously performing poorly), not a one-off lapse. <b>Match to the purpose of each evaluation type:</b> Placement evaluation would only apply before instruction begins; Formative evaluation monitors ongoing progress generally; Summative evaluation only certifies final achievement; none of these three is designed to isolate the <i>root cause</i> of a specific, persistent difficulty. <b>Apply the definition of Diagnostic Evaluation:</b> Diagnostic evaluation is specifically designed to identify the underlying cause(s) of a learner’s persistent learning difficulty in a subject, going deeper than routine formative checks. <b>Conclusion:</b> The scenario exactly matches the purpose of Diagnostic Evaluation – Option 2.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Ram’s problem is not a general, evolving classroom issue (which formative evaluation would catch) nor a start-of-course placement issue – it is a specific, persistent difficulty with one concept. Only diagnostic evaluation is designed to probe <i>why</i> such a difficulty is occurring, so that the teacher can plan targeted remedial teaching. Option 2 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Placement Evaluation) – Incorrect.</b> Placement evaluation is conducted <i>before</i> instruction begins, to decide where a learner should be placed (e.g., which group, level or course); it is not used mid-course to diagnose an existing difficulty.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Diagnostic Evaluation) – Correct.</b> It is precisely the tool used to identify the specific, underlying cause(s) of a learner’s continuing, subject-specific difficulty.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Summative Evaluation) – Incorrect.</b> Summative evaluation happens at the end of a course/unit to grade or certify overall achievement; it does not investigate causes of difficulty, only records final outcomes.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Formative Evaluation) – Incorrect.</b> Formative evaluation provides ongoing feedback during instruction for general improvement, but it is not designed for the deep, cause-finding investigation that a persistent, specific difficulty requires – that is the special role of diagnostic evaluation.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Diagnostic evaluation = finds the root <i>cause</i> of a persistent, specific learning difficulty.</li><li>Placement evaluation = before instruction; Formative = during instruction (ongoing feedback); Summative = at the end (grading/certifying).</li><li>Keyword trigger: ‘continuously performing poorly’ / ‘cause of difficulties’ $\\Rightarrow$ Diagnostic Evaluation.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_c',no:7,q:'Which of the following levels of teaching involves the highest order thinking skills?',opts:['A) Memory level','B) Understanding level','C) Reflective level','D) All levels involve similar thinking skills'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Morrison’s Three Levels of Teaching.</b> <i>Memory level</i> is the lowest – teaching aims only at rote retention/recall of facts (drill, repetition). <i>Understanding level</i> is the middle level – the learner grasps generalisations, relationships, and can apply what is learnt to new but similar situations (comprehension and application). <i>Reflective level</i> is the <b>highest</b> level – it demands independent, critical, problem-solving thought; the learner is expected to analyse, evaluate, and create original insight or solve novel problems, i.e. the highest-order thinking of the three.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Recall the three levels of teaching:</b> Memory (lowest, rote) $\\rightarrow$ Understanding (middle, comprehension/application) $\\rightarrow$ Reflective (highest, critical/creative problem-solving). <b>Compare the cognitive demand of each level:</b> Memory demands only recall; Understanding demands comprehension and application; Reflective demands analysis, evaluation and independent/critical thinking – clearly the highest order. <b>Evaluate Option 4:</b> Since the three levels are explicitly defined as an ascending hierarchy of thinking skill, they cannot all involve <i>similar</i> thinking skills – this rules out Option 4. <b>Conclusion:</b> The Reflective level involves the highest order thinking skills – Option 3.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The three-level model of teaching is explicitly hierarchical, moving from rote recall (Memory) through comprehension/application (Understanding) to independent critical and creative thought (Reflective). By definition, the Reflective level sits at the top of this hierarchy, so Option 3 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Memory level) – Incorrect.</b> This is the <i>lowest</i> level, involving only rote recall/retention – the opposite of what the question asks.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Understanding level) – Incorrect.</b> This is the intermediate level, involving comprehension and application; it is higher than Memory but lower than Reflective.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Reflective level) – Correct.</b> This is the highest level, demanding critical, analytical, and problem-solving (highest-order) thinking.</li><li><b>Option 4 (All levels involve similar thinking skills) – Incorrect.</b> The three levels are defined precisely because they represent <i>different, increasing</i> orders of thinking – they are not similar.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Order (lowest to highest): Memory $\\rightarrow$ Understanding $\\rightarrow$ Reflective.</li><li>Reflective level = highest-order thinking = critical, analytical, problem-solving.</li><li>Do not confuse this three-level model of <i>teaching</i> with Bloom’s six-level taxonomy of <i>cognitive objectives</i>; they are related but separate frameworks.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_c',no:8,q:'Which of the following are the online tools used for creating and sharing mind maps? A. Coggle B. Flipgrid C. Mindmeister D. Google forms E. Miro. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, C &amp; E only','B) B, D, E only','C) A &amp; C only','D) B &amp; D only'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>ICT-based Teaching Support Tools.</b> A mind-mapping tool lets users visually branch out ideas/concepts from a central node and collaborate on that visual map. <i>Coggle</i>, <i>Mindmeister</i> and <i>Miro</i> are all purpose-built online mind-mapping / visual-collaboration whiteboard tools. <i>Flipgrid</i> is a video-discussion platform (students record and reply with short videos), and <i>Google Forms</i> is a survey/data-collection tool – neither is a mind-mapping tool.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>List each tool’s actual primary function:</b> Coggle = mind-mapping; Flipgrid = video-based discussion; Mindmeister = mind-mapping; Google Forms = online survey/quiz creation; Miro = visual collaboration whiteboard (includes mind-mapping). <b>Filter to only mind-mapping/visual-mapping tools:</b> Coggle (A), Mindmeister (C) and Miro (E) qualify; Flipgrid (B) and Google Forms (D) do not. <b>Match to the given option list:</b> The set {A, C, E} corresponds exactly to Option 1. <b>Conclusion:</b> A, C and E only – Option 1.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Among the five tools listed, only Coggle, Mindmeister and Miro are actually designed for creating and sharing mind maps; Flipgrid (video responses) and Google Forms (surveys/quizzes) serve entirely different purposes. Option 1 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A, C &amp; E only) – Correct.</b> Coggle, Mindmeister and Miro are all genuine online mind-mapping/visual-collaboration tools.</li><li><b>Option 2 (B, D, E only) – Incorrect.</b> Wrongly includes Flipgrid (video discussion) and Google Forms (survey tool), neither of which creates mind maps.</li><li><b>Option 3 (A &amp; C only) – Incorrect.</b> Correctly includes Coggle and Mindmeister but incompletely omits Miro, which is also a genuine mind-mapping/visual-whiteboard tool.</li><li><b>Option 4 (B &amp; D only) – Incorrect.</b> Both Flipgrid and Google Forms are unrelated to mind-mapping; this option contains no correct tool at all.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Mind-mapping tools in this list: Coggle, Mindmeister, Miro (A, C, E).</li><li>Flipgrid = video-response discussion tool; Google Forms = survey/quiz tool – both unrelated to mind maps.</li><li>This falls under Unit I’s ‘Teaching Support System: ICT based’ sub-topic – expect similar tool-identification questions across NTA cycles.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_c',no:9,q:'Which of the following is an example of an inclusive learning environment?',opts:['A) A classroom where students are expected to conform to strict set of rules and regulation.','B) A classroom where only students of a certain ethnicity are allowed to participate in class discussion.','C) A classroom where students are discouraged from asking questions or challenging the teacher’s authority.','D) A classroom where all the students are encouraged to share their unique experiences and perspectives.'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Inclusive Learning Environment.</b> Inclusion in education means every learner, regardless of background, ability or identity, is valued, welcomed, and given genuine opportunity to participate and contribute. It is characterised by respect for individual differences, open participation, and the active encouragement of diverse voices and perspectives – the opposite of rigid conformity, exclusion by identity, or suppression of student voice.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Recall the defining feature of inclusion:</b> Valuing diversity and ensuring equitable participation of <i>every</i> learner, with respect for individual differences. <b>Test each option against this definition:</b> Option 1 (strict conformity) suppresses individuality; Option 2 (only one ethnicity may participate) is directly exclusionary; Option 3 (discourages questioning) suppresses student voice/agency; Option 4 (all students encouraged to share unique experiences and perspectives) actively welcomes diversity and participation. <b>Select the option matching inclusion:</b> Only Option 4 reflects the core principle of valuing and inviting every student’s unique background and viewpoint. <b>Conclusion:</b> Option 4 is the example of an inclusive learning environment.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Inclusion is demonstrated by actively inviting and valuing the diverse experiences and perspectives of <i>all</i> students, not by enforcing uniformity, restricting participation by identity, or silencing student voice. Option 4 alone reflects this, so it is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 – Incorrect.</b> Rigid enforcement of a strict set of rules with no room for individual difference works against, not for, inclusion.</li><li><b>Option 2 – Incorrect.</b> Restricting participation to students of a certain ethnicity is the direct opposite of inclusion – it is exclusion on the basis of identity.</li><li><b>Option 3 – Incorrect.</b> Discouraging questions and suppressing challenge to authority denies students voice and agency, which inclusive classrooms actively promote.</li><li><b>Option 4 – Correct.</b> Encouraging every student to share their unique experiences and perspectives is the textbook description of an inclusive classroom.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Inclusion = valuing diversity + equitable participation + respect for individual differences.</li><li>Any option describing exclusion by identity, forced conformity, or suppression of student voice is <i>not</i> inclusive.</li><li>Trigger phrase for the correct answer type: ‘encouraged to share unique experiences and perspectives.’</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_c',no:10,q:'Given below are two statements: Statement I: Teaching at the understanding level involves encouraging students to think critically and reflect on their learning experiences. Statement II: Teaching at the memory level involves rote memorization and does not encourage critical thinking. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are false','C) Statement I is true but Statement II is false','D) Statement I is false but Statement II is true'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Precise Attribution within the Three Levels of Teaching.</b> Memory level = rote recall/retention only, with no demand for critical thinking (a settled, uncontroversial description). Understanding level = comprehension and application of generalisations to new but similar situations – <i>not</i> the level associated with independent critical thinking and reflection on one’s own learning; that description belongs specifically to the <b>Reflective</b> level, the third and highest level in the hierarchy. A statement is only <i>true</i> if it assigns an activity to its <i>correct</i> level – an otherwise accurate description attached to the <i>wrong</i> level name makes the whole statement false.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Evaluate Statement II first (the more settled claim):</b> Memory-level teaching is universally defined as rote memorisation that does not require or encourage critical thinking – this is an accurate, standard description, so Statement II is <b>True</b>. <b>Evaluate Statement I carefully:</b> Encouraging critical thinking and reflection on one’s own learning experience is the hallmark of the <b>Reflective</b> level, not the Understanding level (which is about comprehension/application, a step below reflective, critical thought). <b>Identify the mis-attribution:</b> Statement I takes a real feature of teaching (critical thinking, reflection) but wrongly assigns it to the Understanding level instead of the Reflective level, making Statement I <b>False</b>. <b>Conclusion:</b> Statement I is false, Statement II is true – Option 4.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Statement II correctly and precisely describes memory-level teaching, so it is true. Statement I, however, describes critical thinking and reflection on learning – these are defining features of the Reflective level of teaching, not the Understanding level – so Statement I is false. Since Statement I is false and Statement II is true, Option 4 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Both true) – Incorrect.</b> Statement I mis-assigns a Reflective-level feature to the Understanding level, so it cannot be true.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Both false) – Incorrect.</b> Statement II is a standard, accurate description of memory-level teaching, so it is not false.</li><li><b>Option 3 (I true, II false) – Incorrect.</b> This reverses the correct evaluation; it is Statement I that is false, and Statement II that is true.</li><li><b>Option 4 (I false, II true) – Correct.</b> Statement I wrongly attributes a Reflective-level trait to the Understanding level (false); Statement II accurately describes the Memory level (true).</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Memory level: rote recall, no critical thinking required – Statement II here is correctly worded.</li><li>Understanding level: comprehension and application to new but similar situations – <i>not</i> independent critical reflection.</li><li>Critical thinking and reflection on one’s own learning belong to the <b>Reflective</b> level – watch for statements that borrow this description but misname it as ‘Understanding.’</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 13 Jun 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_d',no:6,q:'Which of the following are not the characteristics of teacher-centered methods of teaching? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> Students are passive learners</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> Teachers act as facilitator for learning</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> Teacher directs learning activities</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> Learning is based on collaboration between students</li><li><span class="p1-lab">E.</span> Learning is based on student interest</li></ol> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, C &amp; D only','B) B &amp; E only','C) B, D &amp; E only','D) A &amp; C only'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept Required</div><div class="p1x-b">This tests the classical contrast between <b>teacher-centred</b> and <b>learner-centred</b> instructional approaches. In teacher-centred teaching, the teacher is the primary authority: knowledge flows one-way from teacher to student, the teacher directs all activities, and students are largely passive recipients. In learner-centred (or student-centred) teaching, the teacher acts as a <i>facilitator</i>, students collaborate with one another, and learning is driven by student interest and active participation.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Classify each statement as a feature of teacher-centred (TC) or learner-centred (LC) teaching.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Statement A – “Students are passive learners” – this IS a genuine characteristic of TC teaching (students absorb information passively).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Statement B – “Teachers act as facilitator” – this is a characteristic of LC teaching, NOT TC teaching (in TC teaching the teacher is an authority/director, not a facilitator).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Statement C – “Teacher directs learning activities” – this IS a genuine characteristic of TC teaching.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Statement D – “Learning is based on collaboration between students” – this is a characteristic of LC teaching, NOT TC teaching (TC teaching is typically individualistic/lecture-based, not collaborative).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Statement E – “Learning is based on student interest” – this is a characteristic of LC teaching, NOT TC teaching (TC teaching follows a fixed syllabus/teacher’s plan, not student interest).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> The statements that are <i>NOT</i> characteristics of teacher-centred teaching are B, D and E – matching <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The question asks which items are <i>not</i> TC characteristics, so the correct set must exclude A and C (which genuinely describe TC teaching) and include only the LC-style features: B (facilitator role), D (peer collaboration) and E (student-interest-driven learning). This is exactly the set given in Option 3.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A, C &amp; D only)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Wrongly includes A and C, which are actual characteristics of teacher-centred teaching (so they should be excluded from a “not characteristics” list), while correctly including D but missing B and E.</li><li><b>Option 2 (B &amp; E only)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Correctly identifies B and E as non-TC features but omits D (collaboration between students), which is equally a learner-centred, not teacher-centred, feature.</li><li><b>Option 3 (B, D &amp; E only)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>Correct</b></span> – Correctly identifies all three learner-centred features (facilitator role, peer collaboration, interest-driven learning) as NOT belonging to teacher-centred teaching.</li><li><b>Option 4 (A &amp; C only)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – This is effectively the reverse/complement of the correct answer – A and C actually ARE teacher-centred characteristics, so this option answers the opposite question (“which ARE” rather than “which are NOT”).</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Teacher-centred (TC): teacher is authority/director, students passive, one-way transmission, fixed content.</li><li>Learner-centred (LC): teacher is facilitator, students collaborate, learning driven by interest/needs.</li><li>A (passive learners) and C (teacher directs) ARE TC features; B (facilitator), D (collaboration) and E (student interest) are NOT.</li><li>Correct answer: B, D &amp; E only – <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_d',no:7,q:'Which of the following is a tool used for creating and sharing online learning games?',opts:['A) Mentimeter','B) Padlet','C) Kahoot','D) Zoom'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept Required</div><div class="p1x-b">This is a direct <b>factual recall</b> question about commonly-used ICT / EdTech tools, part of the “Teaching Support System: Traditional, Modern and ICT based” portion of Unit 1. Candidates are expected to know the primary function of popular classroom-technology platforms.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the primary purpose of each listed tool.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Mentimeter – primarily used for live polls, word clouds and audience-response quizzes during a presentation, not specifically for building shareable “games.”</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Padlet – a collaborative, virtual bulletin/notice board where users post notes, links, images and files; used for brainstorming and content curation, not game creation.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Kahoot – a platform purpose-built for creating game-based, competitive, multiple-choice quizzes (“learning games”) that can be shared with and played live by a class or asynchronously.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Zoom – a video-conferencing platform for live/virtual meetings and classes, not a game-creation tool.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Matching the description “creating and sharing online learning games” most precisely to Kahoot.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> Correct option: <b>Option 3</b> (Kahoot).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Kahoot is specifically designed and marketed as a game-based learning platform: teachers create quiz-style “games” with a scoring/leaderboard system, and students join and play using a game PIN, making it the tool that matches “creating and sharing online learning games” most directly among the four choices.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Mentimeter)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – A live audience-engagement/polling tool (word clouds, live polls, Q&amp;A), not a dedicated game-creation platform.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Padlet)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – A virtual pin-board for collaborative content sharing and organisation; useful for brainstorming, not for building competitive learning games.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Kahoot)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>Correct</b></span> – Purpose-built game-based quiz platform; matches the question exactly.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Zoom)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – A video-conferencing tool for synchronous online classes/meetings; unrelated to game creation.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Kahoot $\\to$ game-based quizzes and learning games.</li><li>Mentimeter $\\to$ live polls, word clouds, audience response.</li><li>Padlet $\\to$ collaborative virtual notice board.</li><li>Zoom $\\to$ video conferencing.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_d',no:8,q:'What are the various salient features of formative evaluation? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> It provides feedback to the teachers to use teaching strategies according to the needs of the learners</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> It helps learners to actively and continuously engage in learning</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> It helps in enhancing the learning abilities of the learner</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> It contributes to the overall grading and certification of the learner</li><li><span class="p1-lab">E.</span> It ‘sums-up’ how much a student has learnt over a period of time.</li></ol> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) B, D &amp; E only','B) C &amp; E only','C) A, B &amp; C only','D) A &amp; D only'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept Required</div><div class="p1x-b">This tests the distinction between <b>formative evaluation</b> (ongoing, diagnostic, feedback-oriented, conducted <i>during</i> the learning process to improve teaching and learning) and <b>summative evaluation</b> (conducted <i>at the end</i> of a course/unit to grade, certify and “sum up” overall achievement).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Classify each statement as a feature of formative (F) or summative (S) evaluation.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Statement A – feedback to teachers to adjust strategies to learner needs – this is a core <b>formative</b> feature (formative evaluation exists precisely to inform ongoing teaching decisions).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Statement B – helps learners actively and continuously engage – this is a core <b>formative</b> feature (continuous engagement is definitional to formative, ongoing assessment).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Statement C – helps enhance learning abilities of the learner – this is a <b>formative</b> feature (formative evaluation’s purpose is improvement of learning, not just measurement).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Statement D – contributes to overall grading and certification – this is a <b>summative</b> feature (grading/certification happens at the end, not during, learning).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Statement E – “sums up” how much a student has learnt over a period – this is, by its own wording, a <b>summative</b> feature (the word “sums-up” is a direct signal of summative evaluation).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> The salient features of formative evaluation are A, B and C – matching <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Formative evaluation is continuous and improvement-oriented: it gives teachers real-time feedback to adjust their strategies (A), keeps learners actively engaged throughout the learning process (B), and directly enhances learning ability through timely correction (C). Grading/certification (D) and “summing up” total learning (E) are, by definition, functions of summative evaluation, conducted at the end of instruction – so only A, B and C correctly describe formative evaluation.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (B, D &amp; E only)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Correctly includes B but wrongly includes D and E, which are summative-evaluation features, and wrongly excludes A and C.</li><li><b>Option 2 (C &amp; E only)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Correctly includes C but wrongly includes E (a summative feature) and misses A and B.</li><li><b>Option 3 (A, B &amp; C only)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>Correct</b></span> – Correctly isolates all three genuine formative-evaluation features and excludes both summative features (D, E).</li><li><b>Option 4 (A &amp; D only)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Correctly includes A but wrongly includes D (grading/certification is summative), and misses B and C.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Formative evaluation: continuous, diagnostic, feedback-driven, aims to <i>improve</i> learning while it is happening (A, B, C).</li><li>Summative evaluation: end-of-course, grading/certification, “sums up” total achievement (D, E).</li><li>Keyword trigger: “sums-up” in a statement almost always signals <i>summative</i>, not formative, evaluation.</li><li>Correct answer: A, B &amp; C only – <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_d',no:9,q:'Given below are two statements:<br><br>Statement I: A learner’s cultural background has no impact on his/her learning experience.<br><br>Statement II: Gender is not a strong predictor of academic achievement.<br><br>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are false','C) Statement I is true but Statement II is false','D) Statement I is false but Statement II is true'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept Required</div><div class="p1x-b">This tests understanding of <b>learner characteristics</b> – specifically, the well-established educational-psychology positions that (a) cultural background <i>does</i> significantly shape a learner’s experience (through language, prior knowledge, values, learning style preferences, and classroom interaction norms), and (b) contemporary educational research finds that gender, by itself, is <i>not</i> a strong or reliable predictor of academic achievement once other factors (opportunity, motivation, teaching quality, socio-economic context) are accounted for.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Evaluate Statement I – “A learner’s cultural background has no impact on his/her learning experience.”</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Cultural background shapes prior knowledge, language proficiency, values, communication styles and even comfort with certain teaching methods; it demonstrably <i>does</i> impact learning experience. Hence Statement I is <b>False</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Evaluate Statement II – “Gender is not a strong predictor of academic achievement.”</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Modern educational research (and NCERT/NCTE-aligned teacher-education material) holds that academic achievement depends far more on factors such as motivation, effort, teaching quality, and opportunity than on gender itself; hence gender alone is <i>not</i> a strong predictor. Statement II is <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Combine: Statement I is False, Statement II is True.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Match to the options – “Statement I is false but Statement II is true.”</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> Correct option: <b>Option 4</b>.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The correct pedagogical position recognises cultural background as a major, well-documented influence on how a learner experiences and engages with education (making Statement I false), while treating gender as <i>not</i> inherently determinative of academic outcomes in itself (making Statement II true). This combination – I false, II true – corresponds exactly to Option 4.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Both true)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Wrongly treats Statement I as true; cultural background is, in fact, a well-recognised influence on learning experience.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Both false)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Wrongly treats Statement II as false; contemporary research supports that gender alone is not a strong predictor of achievement.</li><li><b>Option 3 (I true, II false)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – This is the exact reverse of the correct pairing; it wrongly denies the influence of culture and wrongly asserts gender as a strong predictor.</li><li><b>Option 4 (I false, II true)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>Correct</b></span> – Correctly identifies that culture does matter (Statement I false) and gender alone is not a strong predictor (Statement II true).</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Cultural background <i>does</i> influence learning experience (language, prior knowledge, values, interaction style) – Statement I is False.</li><li>Gender alone is <i>not</i> a strong predictor of academic achievement in contemporary educational psychology – Statement II is True.</li><li>Combination: I False, II True – <b>Option 4</b>.</li><li>This is a classic “two-statement evaluation” item – assess each statement completely independently before combining.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u1',paper:'jun23_d',no:10,q:'Match List I with List II<br><br><div class="p1-blk"><div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th colspan="2"><b>LIST I (Three Approaches of Education)</b></th><th colspan="2"><b>LIST II (Disciplinary aspects)</b></th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>Teacher-centred</td><td>I.</td><td>Shared, Participatory</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>Learner-centred</td><td>II.</td><td>Imposed</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>Learning-centred</td><td>III.</td><td>Self-controlled</td></tr></table></div></div><br><br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-III, B-II, C-I','B) A-I, B-II, C-III','C) A-II, B-I, C-III','D) A-II, B-III, C-I'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept Required</div><div class="p1x-b">This question tests the finer three-way classification of educational approaches taught in Unit 1 – <b>Teacher-centred</b>, <b>Learner-centred</b>, and <b>Learning-centred</b> – each linked to a distinct disciplinary character. Teacher-centred approaches are characterised by discipline being <i>imposed</i> from above by the teacher/institution. Learner-centred approaches emphasise the learner’s active, <i>shared and participatory</i> role alongside the teacher in the learning process. Learning-centred approaches, which focus on the learning process itself and on learners regulating their own study, are characterised by <i>self-controlled</i>, self-directed discipline.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the defining disciplinary character of each of the three approaches.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Teacher-centred (A): the teacher is the sole authority who dictates rules and content; discipline is <b>Imposed</b> (II) on the learner from outside.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Learner-centred (B): the learner actively participates and shares responsibility with the teacher for the learning process; discipline/engagement is <b>Shared, Participatory</b> (I).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Learning-centred (C): the focus shifts to the learning process itself, with the learner regulating and directing their own study habits; discipline is <b>Self-controlled</b> (III).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Assemble the mapping: A-II, B-I, C-III.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Compare against the four options.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> A-II, B-I, C-III matches <b>Option 3</b> exactly.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Each of the three educational approaches carries a distinct disciplinary flavour that flows directly from who controls the learning process: Teacher-centred = imposed control (teacher decides everything), Learner-centred = shared/participatory control (teacher and learner jointly engage), and Learning-centred = self-controlled (the learner internalises responsibility for the process). This precise correspondence (A-II, B-I, C-III) is captured only in Option 3.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A-III, B-II, C-I)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Reverses Teacher-centred and Learning-centred’s disciplinary character (wrongly makes Teacher-centred “self-controlled” and Learner-centred “imposed”), the opposite of their actual definitions.</li><li><b>Option 2 (A-I, B-II, C-III)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Wrongly assigns “Shared, Participatory” to Teacher-centred (which is actually imposed/authoritarian) and “Imposed” to Learner-centred (which is actually participatory).</li><li><b>Option 3 (A-II, B-I, C-III)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>Correct</b></span> – Correctly pairs Teacher-centred with Imposed, Learner-centred with Shared/Participatory, and Learning-centred with Self-controlled.</li><li><b>Option 4 (A-II, B-III, C-I)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Correctly pairs A with II (Teacher-centred = Imposed) but swaps the pairings for Learner-centred and Learning-centred, wrongly making Learner-centred “self-controlled” and Learning-centred “shared/participatory.”</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Teacher-centred $\\to$ <b>Imposed</b> discipline (teacher-driven authority).</li><li>Learner-centred $\\to$ <b>Shared, Participatory</b> discipline (teacher and learner jointly engaged).</li><li>Learning-centred $\\to$ <b>Self-controlled</b> discipline (learner self-regulates the learning process).</li><li>Correct mapping: A-II, B-I, C-III – <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'}

);
